window.VICU_GUIDE = [{"s":"","t":"The Vicu guide","d":"How to use Vicu, the task manager for your own Vikunja server, on Windows, macOS, Linux, Android, iPhone and iPad. From the first sign-in to Vicu Pro.","h":[["how-this-guide-works","How this guide works"],["start-here","Start here"],["everyday-vicu","Everyday Vicu"],["your-devices","Your devices"],["vicu-pro","Vicu Pro"],["reference","Reference"],["about-vicu","About Vicu"]],"x":"How this guide works The guide goes from the big picture to the details. Each page opens with In short , a few lines that are often all you need, then the steps, then the finer points. Badges show where something works: . Search with the box on the left, or press / . Start here Everyday Vicu Your devices Vicu Pro Vicu stays simple for everyone. Vicu Pro is an optional switch for more complex work: what you can do right now, promises people made you, initiatives you steer, and faster ways through many tasks. Pro only adds; with it off, Vicu is the app you know. New to it? Watch the five-minute tour guide:pro watch-the-tour . Reference About Vicu Vicu is a free, open-source task manager for Vikunja https://vikunja.io/ , the open-source to-do server you host yourself. Your tasks live on your server, not with Vicu. Vicu is an independent app, not made by the Vikunja team. Found something that is wrong or missing here? Each page has a link at the bottom to suggest a change, or open an issue https://github.com/rendyhd/Vicu/issues ."},{"s":"getting-started","t":"Get started","d":"Install Vicu on Windows, macOS, Linux, Android or iPhone, connect it to your Vikunja server, choose your Inbox and add your first tasks.","h":[["in-short","In short"],["what-you-need-first","What you need first"],["install-vicu","Install Vicu"],["windows","Windows"],["macos","macOS"],["linux","Linux"],["android","Android"],["iphone-and-ipad","iPhone and iPad"],["connect-to-your-server","Connect to your server"],["sign-in-with-a-username-and-password","Sign in with a username and password"],["sign-in-with-single-sign-on","Sign in with single sign-on"],["sign-in-with-an-api-token","Sign in with an API token"],["when-your-provider-asks-for-a-passkey","When your provider asks for a passkey"],["on-a-phone","On a phone"],["choose-your-inbox","Choose your Inbox"],["add-your-first-tasks","Add your first tasks"],["switching-account-or-server","Switching account or server"],["where-to-go-next","Where to go next"],["related","Related"]],"x":"In short - Vicu needs a Vikunja server. It has no server or account of its own. Use your own server or Vikunja Cloud, version 2.4.0 or newer. - Install the app for your device, open it and enter your server address. - Sign in with a username and password, single sign-on, or an API token. - Choose your Inbox , the project where new tasks land. It syncs, so your other devices use the same one. - Add a task with the + button or Ctrl + N Cmd on a Mac , then look at Today . What you need first Vicu shows and edits the tasks on a Vikunja https://vikunja.io/ server. Before you install, have these ready: - The address of your server, for example https://app.vikunja.cloud . - A way to sign in: a username and password, a single sign-on account, or an API token. Vicu collects nothing. Your tasks go to your server and nowhere else. Everything else you turn on, such as the assistant or a calendar, is optional. Install Vicu Windows Download the installer from the latest release https://github.com/rendyhd/Vicu/releases/latest and run it. It works on Windows 10 and 11 x64 . The installer is not code-signed, so Windows SmartScreen may stop it. Choose More info , then Run anyway . macOS 1. Download the .dmg from the latest release https://github.com/rendyhd/Vicu/releases/latest . It needs macOS 13 or newer on an Apple silicon Mac. 2. Open it and drag Vicu into Applications . Linux Vicu comes as an AppImage for x64 and arm64. 1. Download the file from the latest release https://github.com/rendyhd/Vicu/releases/latest . 2. Make it runnable with chmod +x Vicu- .AppImage . 3. Run it. The AppImage needs no extra libraries. It also carries update information, so tools such as AppImageUpdate or Gear Lever can update it. On Wayland, accept the GlobalShortcuts dialog if one appears. If the global shortcuts still do not work, Settings, Quick Entry / View shows commands you can bind in your desktop's keyboard settings. On GNOME, the tray icon needs the AppIndicator and KStatusNotifierItem Support extension. On Wayland, accept the GlobalShortcuts dialog if one appears. If the global shortcuts still do not work, Settings, Quick Entry / View shows commands you can bind in your desktop's keyboard settings. On GNOME, the tray icon needs the AppIndicator and KStatusNotifierItem Support extension. Android Vicu for Android needs Android 8 or newer. Get it from Google Play as a beta, through Obtainium, or as an APK from the mobile releases page https://github.com/rendyhd/Vicu-Mobile/releases/latest . The Play build and the APK are the same signed app. The Android download section https://github.com/rendyhd/Vicu-Mobile download has the current steps for joining the beta. iPhone and iPad Vicu needs iOS or iPadOS 17 or newer. It is a public beta on TestFlight https://testflight.apple.com/join/c6wuvXrH . Install TestFlight first if you do not have it, then open the link on your device. Connect to your server Vicu checks the server first and shows only the sign-in methods it offers. Sign in with a username and password Choose Sign in with username & password . Enter your Username or Email and Password . If you use two-factor authentication, Vicu then asks for the 6-digit code from your authenticator app. Sign in with single sign-on If your server has single sign-on, you see a Sign in with button named for your provider. Vicu opens its own sign-in window, and you sign in there as you would on the web. Vicu needs nothing set up at the provider for this. Sign in with an API token Choose Use API Token instead and paste a token from Vikunja. The setup screen has an info button that lists the permissions the token needs. A token that lacks a permission can sign in but fails later on comments, assignees or sharing; Vicu then tells you which permission is missing. A mistyped token no longer signs you out on the phone. When your provider asks for a passkey Some providers, such as Pocket ID, sign you in with a passkey. A passkey cannot complete inside Vicu's own sign-in window on macOS and Linux. When Vicu sees the request, it closes the window and offers Sign in through your browser . You can also choose Uses a passkey? Sign in through your browser yourself. The browser sign-in needs one setting at your provider, once: 1. Copy the redirect address Vicu shows with the Copy button. On the desktop it is http://127.0.0.1:47823/vicu/oidc-callback . 2. Add it as a redirect callback URL to the Vikunja client at your identity provider. 3. Choose Sign in through your browser again and finish in your browser. Vicu remembers which way you signed in and uses the same way when it asks you to sign in again. On Windows you do not need this, because Windows Hello handles passkeys. On a phone The phone apps use the same methods. The setup remembers the server address, and Back steps through the screens. Tokens and passwords stay hidden behind a show toggle, and an http:// address outside your own network gets a warning. On iPhone the single sign-on page opens in an in-app web view, which Google and some providers refuse. If that happens, use Sign in through your browser , a password or an API token. The browser redirect address on phones is com.rendyhd.vicu:/oidc-callback . After setup, the phone asks to send notifications and explains why. Choose your Inbox The Inbox is the project where new tasks land before you sort them. The last step of setup asks you to choose it. - If another device already chose an Inbox, Vicu suggests that one. - Otherwise Vicu suggests your server's default project, or a project named Inbox. - You can change it later in Settings, Projects . A project cannot be put inside the Inbox. The Inbox choice syncs to all your devices. Add your first tasks 1. Press the + button, or Ctrl + N on the desktop. On the phone, tap + . 2. Type Call the dentist tomorrow 3pm . 3. Press Enter . Vicu reads the date and time from the words and shows them as chips. See Quick add guide:quick-add for everything it understands. Then open Today to see what is due. When you finish a task, tick its checkbox; Vicu keeps it on screen for a few seconds and offers Undo . Switching account or server Signing in as a different user, or on a different server, clears the previous account's custom lists, Inbox choice and synced settings from the device. Device settings such as the theme and shortcuts stay. If something has not reached the server yet, Vicu asks before signing you out. Where to go next - Read How Vicu is organized guide:basics for the big picture. - Learn the ways to add tasks guide:capture . - On the desktop, turn on Quick Entry in Settings, Quick Entry / View to add tasks from any app. - Keep the guide handy: the FAQ guide:faq covers sign-in and sync problems. Related - How Vicu is organized guide:basics - Add tasks guide:capture - Quick add guide:quick-add - Sync and offline guide:sync - Troubleshooting and FAQ guide:faq"},{"s":"basics","t":"How Vicu is organized","d":"The ideas behind Vicu: the Inbox, smart lists, projects, sections, categories, labels and custom lists, and a short tour of the main window and the phone layout.","h":[["in-short","In short"],["the-idea","The idea"],["the-pieces","The pieces"],["projects-subprojects-and-sections","Projects, subprojects and sections"],["categories","Categories"],["labels","Labels"],["custom-lists","Custom lists"],["the-task-card","The task card"],["a-tour-of-the-main-window","A tour of the main window"],["a-tour-of-the-phone","A tour of the phone"],["what-syncs-and-what-stays-on-one-device","What syncs and what stays on one device"],["related","Related"]],"x":"In short - New tasks land in the Inbox. You sort them later, or not at all. - Smart lists answer \"when\". Today, Upcoming, Anytime and Logbook are built from your tasks' dates. You do not fill them by hand. - Projects answer \"where\". Projects can hold subprojects and sections, and top-level projects can be grouped under categories. - Labels cut across projects. A label such as errand can be on tasks in any project. - Custom lists are your own views. A list is a saved set of conditions, and it is the same on every device. - Everything is stored on your Vikunja server. Vicu is a window onto it. The idea A task starts in the Inbox. You give it a day, and it shows up in Today when that day comes. When you finish it, it goes to the Logbook. You do not have to file it in a project first, and Vicu has no boards or Gantt charts. Projects, labels and custom lists exist for the times when you want to look at your work by topic or by a rule of your own. The pieces Piece What it is Where you find it --- --- --- Inbox The project where new tasks land. You choose it once, and it syncs. First in the sidebar or drawer Today Everything due before tomorrow starts: overdue and due today Smart list Upcoming Everything due from tomorrow on, grouped by day Smart list Anytime Every open task outside the Inbox, with or without a date Smart list Logbook Tasks you have completed, by the day you finished them Smart list Projects Groups of tasks, which can hold subprojects and sections Projects in the sidebar or drawer Categories Named headers that group your top-level projects Between projects in the sidebar Labels Colored tags you put on tasks Tags in the sidebar; Labels in the phone drawer Custom lists Your own lists made from conditions Under the smart lists Read Smart lists guide:lists for the five lists, Projects and labels guide:projects for the organization tools, and Custom lists guide:custom-lists for lists of your own. Projects, subprojects and sections A project can hold other projects. A top-level project acts like an area, with the projects below it. Inside a project, sections split a long list into collapsible groups with headers and counts. The Inbox does not appear among the projects, because it has its own place at the top. Categories If you have many top-level projects, you can group them under named headers called categories. They are optional. Without a category, the sidebar is a plain list of projects. Labels A label is a colored tag. Each label has its own page that shows every task with that label, across all projects. You can put several labels on one task. Custom lists A custom list is a saved view: for example \"high priority tasks due this week in Work\". It is stored on your server, so every device shows the same list. See Custom lists guide:custom-lists . The task card Everything about one task lives in its card on the desktop, or in the task editor on a phone. It holds the title, notes, dates, reminders, repeat, priority, labels, subtasks, checklists and files. Properties you have set show as chips. Properties you have not set show as quiet buttons you can click to add them. See Work with tasks guide:tasks . A tour of the main window The desktop window has a sidebar on the left and the content on the right. - Top of the sidebar: the search box, called Quick find, with the Ask button next to it when the assistant is set up. - Smart lists: Inbox, Today, Upcoming, Anytime and Logbook. Routines, Review and Plan appear here when you turn them on. - Custom lists , and a + button to make one. - Projects , in a tree, with progress rings. Subprojects fold under their parent. - Tags: your labels. - The sync button , which opens a panel with changes that are waiting to reach the server. Click a task to open its card inside the list. Press Escape to close it. Right-click a task for its menu. Press Ctrl + Shift + P Cmd on a Mac to open the command palette, where you can jump to any list or run any command. ! The Inbox on the desktop screen:desktop-inbox.webp \"The sidebar on the left, the Inbox on the right.\" A tour of the phone The phone has the same pieces in a different layout. - The bottom bar holds the Inbox first, then three slots you choose: smart lists, projects or custom lists. - The drawer opens from the menu button and lists everything: smart lists, custom lists, projects and labels. Smart lists you hide in settings disappear from it. - The + button adds a task. Hold it to say a task. - A task opens as a sheet over the list. Pull it down to close it. - Swipe a row to complete it or to choose a day. You can change what each swipe does in Settings, Gestures . - Search is at the top of every list, with the Ask button next to it when the assistant is set up. ! Today on Android screen:android-today-light.webp \"Today on Android.\" What syncs and what stays on one device Your tasks, projects, labels and comments live on your server. Vicu also saves some of its own data there, in a way other Vikunja apps do not show you: custom lists, the Inbox choice, project categories and order, templates and routines. These follow you to every device. Choices about how the app looks stay on the device: the theme, the clock, hotkeys and which smart lists show. Sync and offline guide:sync and Settings guide:settings have the full lists. Vicu keeps some data in tasks it hides, such as custom lists and routines. If you open your Vikunja server in another app, you may see tasks with names like \"Vicu preferences sync metadata — do not delete \". Leave them alone. Vicu keeps some data in tasks it hides, such as custom lists and routines. If you open your Vikunja server in another app, you may see tasks with names like \"Vicu preferences sync metadata — do not delete \". Leave them alone. Related - Get started guide:getting-started - Add tasks guide:capture - Smart lists guide:lists - Projects and labels guide:projects - Custom lists guide:custom-lists"},{"s":"capture","t":"Add tasks","d":"Every way to add a task in Vicu: the new task row, Quick Entry, your voice, the share sheet, widgets, pasted lists, templates and duplicates.","h":[["in-short","In short"],["add-a-task-in-the-app","Add a task in the app"],["on-the-desktop","On the desktop"],["on-a-phone","On a phone"],["quick-entry","Quick Entry"],["say-a-task","Say a task"],["from-other-apps-and-your-phone","From other apps and your phone"],["paste-several-tasks","Paste several tasks"],["paste-markdown-into-notes","Paste Markdown into notes"],["reuse-a-task","Reuse a task"],["duplicate","Duplicate"],["templates","Templates"],["copy-titles","Copy titles"],["related","Related"]],"x":"In short - Type it. The new task row at the end of a list, Ctrl + N Cmd on a Mac or the phone's + button. Words like tomorrow 3pm or work become dates and projects. - Quick Entry is a small window you open with a global hotkey, over any app. - Say it. Hold + and speak. No setup needed. - Share it from any app, or use a widget, a launcher shortcut or Siri. - Paste a list and get one task per line. - Reuse a task with templates and Duplicate. New tasks go to the list you are looking at when that makes sense, and to the Inbox otherwise. Add a task in the app On the desktop 1. Click New task at the end of a list, or press Ctrl + N . You can also use File, New task . 2. Type the title. Vicu highlights what it recognizes and shows it as chips. 3. Press Enter . ! The task composer on the desktop screen:crop-composer.webp \"Recognized words become chips.\" Each chip can be removed, and suggestions for projects, labels and dates appear under your cursor as you type. Chips follow what you type unless you set them yourself. The rest of the text is the title. Quick add guide:quick-add lists everything Vicu reads, including the choice between the Todoist-style syntax @label , project and the Vikunja-style syntax label , +project in Settings, General, Task Parser . In a project, the task goes to that project. In Today, it is due today. In a custom list, it goes to the list's Add tasks to project. In the Inbox, Upcoming, Anytime and a label page, it goes to the Inbox, without a date unless you type one. Type ! at the start or end of the title to make the task due today. Add for 30m after a time, as in Call Sam tomorrow at 3pm for 30m , to make a time block. See Plan your day guide:plan . On a phone 1. Tap + . One sheet opens with the keyboard. 2. Type the task. Dates, projects, labels and repeats move into chips as you type. 3. Tap Add . A row of chips under the title holds When , Remind and Priority , plus tags, project and repeat once they are set. Tap a chip to choose from options where the keyboard was. Choose Expand , or drag the handle up, for the full editor with notes and a checklist; checklist items become subtasks. Nothing is saved until you tap Add . The X clears the draft and turns into Undo . The confirmation after adding has Open next to Undo . If you want the sheet to stay open for several tasks in a row, turn on Keep add-task open in settings. You can also drag + onto a day or a project in Today, Upcoming, Anytime or a project, and quick add opens with that date or project already set. Quick Entry Quick Entry is a small always-on-top window you open from any app with a global hotkey. Type, press Enter , and it is gone. It is off until you turn it on in Settings, Quick Entry / View . The default hotkey is Alt + Shift + V Cmd + Shift + Space on a Mac . You can record your own there. ! Quick Entry screen:desktop-quick-entry.webp \"Quick Entry over another app.\" - Hold a modifier key and press the arrow keys to switch the project. - Add notes under the title. Paragraphs are kept. - Press Ctrl + L to link the current Obsidian note or the current browser tab to the task. Set both up in Settings, Quick Entry / View . Obsidian detection is not available on Linux. - It works offline. The task is queued and sent when you are back online. Quick View is the companion window: a hotkey Alt + Shift + B by default that shows any list so you can check things off. See On the desktop guide:desktop . On Linux with Wayland, global hotkeys depend on your desktop. See Get started guide:getting-started for the one-time step. On Linux with Wayland, global hotkeys depend on your desktop. See Get started guide:getting-started for the one-time step. Say a task Voice capture works without any assistant. 1. Hold the + button for a moment. 2. Speak. Your words show live, with the date, project and labels highlighted. 3. Let go to add the task. Slide away, or press Esc on the desktop, to cancel. A short click or tap on + still opens the normal composer. The words are read like quick add, and the task goes to the list you are looking at. A project or date you say wins over the list's default. The default reminder applies. On the desktop you can also record a Voice input shortcut in Settings, Keyboard Shortcuts . Hold it to talk, or tap it to keep listening until you press it again. It works only while Vicu is in front. The command palette's Say a task starts listening without holding anything. Voice works with the platform's own speech recognizer: Apple's on a Mac, Windows speech recognition on Windows, and the phone's recognizer on Android and iPhone. On Linux, + opens the composer instead, because there is no platform recognizer. Sliding up from the held + keeps the microphone open and hands your words to smart dictation, which can make several tasks from one stretch of talk. See The assistant guide:assistant . From other apps and your phone Way What it does Where --- --- --- Share to Vicu Share a link, photo or file from any app. Android opens the full task entry. iPhone shows a small sheet with title, notes and attachments, and the task lands in the Inbox the next time Vicu runs. Quick add widget A 2x1 widget with a microphone and a + button Tasks widget Any list on your home screen, with tap to complete, plus a microphone and + on Android Routines widget Today's routines, tap to log Launcher shortcuts Hold the app icon, then choose Add task or Dictate Quick Settings tile Quick add from the notification shade Siri \"Hey Siri, add a task in Vicu\". It asks \"What's the task?\" and works on a locked phone. Control Center and Lock Screen An Add task control on iOS 18, which you can also put on the Action Button A task shared from another app is not read for dates on iPhone. When you share text, you can choose Make tasks to turn it into task cards if the assistant is set up. See On your phone guide:phone for widgets and shortcuts in detail. Paste several tasks Copy a list of lines and press Ctrl + V in a list, or use New task from clipboard . Vicu makes one task per line. Each line is read like quick add. Paste Markdown into notes Markdown you paste into a task's notes becomes formatting. Typing works too. See Work with tasks guide:tasks notes . Reuse a task Duplicate Duplicate makes a new open task in the same project with the title, notes, dates, priority, repeat, reminders, labels, assignees and subtasks. A time block is kept too. - On the desktop: right-click the task and choose Duplicate , use the card's More menu, or press Ctrl + D . - On a phone: use the task menu or the selection menu. A message offers Open for a single copy and Undo , which deletes every copy. Created offline, a desktop copy comes without its subtasks and says so. Templates A template is a task you want again and again. 1. Open the task's menu and choose Save as template . It keeps the project, notes, labels, priority, repeat, subtasks and reminders. A due date is kept as a number of days from today. 2. In a new task row, or in the phone's quick add, use the last button, Template , to see your templates. 3. Choose one. Vicu makes the task and opens it with the title selected. Open the arrow beside a template on the desktop, or press and hold it on a phone, for Edit and Remove . Templates sync to all your devices. Copy titles Select tasks and press Ctrl + C to copy their titles, one per line. On a phone, use Copy titles in the selection menu. Related - Quick add guide:quick-add - Work with tasks guide:tasks - On the desktop guide:desktop - On your phone guide:phone - The assistant guide:assistant"},{"s":"tasks","t":"Work with tasks","d":"Everything in a Vicu task: dates, reminders, repeats, priority, labels, notes, subtasks, files, completing and undoing, deleting and working on many tasks at once.","h":[["in-short","In short"],["open-and-close-a-task","Open and close a task"],["on-the-desktop","On the desktop"],["on-a-phone","On a phone"],["dates-and-the-when-panel","Dates and the When panel"],["reminders","Reminders"],["default-reminder","Default reminder"],["place-reminders","Place reminders"],["when-a-reminder-fires","When a reminder fires"],["repeats","Repeats"],["priority","Priority"],["labels","Labels"],["notes","Notes"],["markdown","Markdown"],["long-notes","Long notes"],["images","Images"],["subtasks-and-checklists","Subtasks and checklists"],["attachments","Attachments"],["comments-and-assignees","Comments and assignees"],["complete-undo-and-delete","Complete, undo and delete"],["complete","Complete"],["delete","Delete"],["duplicate-and-templates","Duplicate and templates"],["select-several-tasks","Select several tasks"],["on-the-desktop-2","On the desktop"],["on-a-phone-2","On a phone"],["move-drag-and-reorder","Move, drag and reorder"],["row-density","Row density"],["swipe-actions","Swipe actions"],["print","Print"],["related","Related"]],"x":"In short - Open a task to see its card. Set properties show as chips; unset ones are quiet buttons you can click to add. - The When panel sets every date and time in one place. Type a date in your own words, or pick one. - Complete with the checkbox. The task stays for a few seconds, and Undo brings it back. - Notes are rich text. You can paste or type Markdown and it becomes formatting. - Select several tasks to schedule, move, label, complete or delete them together. Open and close a task On the desktop Click a task, or press Enter on a focused row. The row grows into a card inside the list. Edits save as you go. - Esc closes the card and saves. - Ctrl + Enter saves and closes Cmd on a Mac . - Enter in the title moves to the notes. ! A task card on the desktop screen:desktop-card.webp \"Chips for what is set, quiet buttons for the rest.\" On a phone Tap a task. A sheet opens over the list with an editable headline, the notes, and the properties in one card. Properties you have not set sit under Add . Pull the sheet down to close it. It saves as you type. ! The task editor on iPhone screen:ios-task-editor-light.webp Dates and the When panel Choose the date chip, or the date button on a phone, to open the When panel. It sets every date and time: - Type a date in your own words, such as friday or in 3 days . - Use the quick choices, which name the coming weekdays. - Pick from the month calendar. On the desktop, use the arrow keys, Home , End and Page Up or Page Down . The week starts on Monday. - Choose a time. A custom time applies when you press Enter . ! The When panel screen:crop-when.webp On a phone, the editor also has one-tap Today , Tomorrow , This weekend and Next week buttons, with Undo . A date without a time counts as the end of that day and shows without a time. A date with a time keeps it. On the desktop you can press Ctrl + T to set the due date to today. Dates read the same everywhere: Today, Tomorrow, weekdays, then dates in your system's format. Chips say the word and the date for nearby days, such as \"Today, 7 Oct\". If a task has a time block, which is a start and end time on one day, it shows as a time range. See Plan your day guide:plan . Reminders Choose the bell to add a reminder. A reminder can be an exact time, or relative to a date, such as \"1 hour before\" the due date. On the desktop you can count from the due, start or end date. Default reminder You can have Vicu add a reminder to every new task that has a due date and no reminder of its own. It applies to the composer, Quick Entry, pasted lines and voice. It never changes existing tasks. Set it in Settings, Notifications, Default reminder on the desktop, or on the Notifications tab on a phone. You choose off, at the due time, or a time before. With a time block, it goes by the block's start. Place reminders A phone can remind you when you arrive at or leave a place. - Save places in Settings, Notifications, Places on the phone, or with Save as place . Only the place's name syncs. Where it is stays on that phone. - On a task, choose At a location... in the reminder menu, then pick a place, a searched address or where you are now, and a radius of 100, 200 or 500 meters. - Vicu needs location access set to Always , and explains this first. On the desktop you can read your places in Settings, Notifications and set a reminder at one with At a place . Only phones fire place reminders. Typing when I get home in quick add sets one for a saved place. When a reminder fires On a phone, the notification has Mark Complete and Snooze 15 min . On the desktop, a notification brings Vicu forward when you click it. Reminders for tasks done or deleted on another device do not ring. Repeats Choose the repeat chip, or type every friday , every 3 days or daily in quick add. Any interval works. Completing a repeating task moves its dates to the next time. Undo puts the dates back. Priority Choose the priority chip. Priority shows as marks: one, two or three bars for low, medium and high, and a filled square with an exclamation mark for urgent. The name shows on hover and to screen readers. On the desktop, Settings, General, What does urgent mean? decides the top action in the right-click menu: Schedule for Today, or Mark Important. Labels Choose the label chip to add labels. On the phone, you can create a label right from the picker. A drag onto a label in the sidebar also applies it on the desktop. See Projects and labels guide:projects labels . Notes Notes are rich text. You can use links, checklists, bold and italic, lists, and pasted images. Notes are saved in Vikunja's own format, so other Vikunja apps show them too. Markdown Markdown you paste becomes formatting, and typing it formats as you go: headings, quotes, lists, checklists, dividers, bold, italic, strikethrough, code and text url links. - Press Ctrl + Shift + V to paste without conversion. - On iPhone, pasted Markdown stays plain text for now. Typing it works. Long notes A long note folds at about half the visible height, with Show all . On the desktop, Alt + Up and Alt + Down move a list line up or down. On a phone, the toolbar has the same, and Copy notes is in the menu. Images Paste an image into the notes on the desktop and on Android. On iPhone, use Add image . Click or tap a picture in the notes, or an image attachment, to open the viewer: - Fit the image or see it at its own size. - Press Left and Right to go through the task's images. - On the desktop, Copy or Ctrl+C, Cmd+C on a Mac puts the image on the clipboard, ready to paste anywhere. - Open in default app and Save are there too. HEIC and TIFF images open in your default app instead. Subtasks and checklists Add subtasks in the task card. Add several in a row. Click a subtask's title to rename it. Right-click it for Rename , Open subtask and Delete subtask . Open subtask opens it as its own card. Deleting asks whether to delete nested subtasks too, when you have confirmation on. On the desktop, Settings, General, Subtasks in task lists chooses whether subtasks show Inside task or are Expandable in lists. To change the order of subtasks, drag the grip that appears on hover, or use Move up and Move down in the row's menu. On a phone, press and hold. The order is yours and syncs. Other Vikunja apps keep the server's order. A checklist in the phone's quick add becomes subtasks. Attachments Add files from the attachment button. Any file type works. Each file offers Open and Save , and the phone adds Share . Deleting a file asks first. Files you add while offline wait in the queue and upload when the server is back. The attachment list shows what is waiting, with Retry and Remove . Images land at the end of the notes. Comments and assignees Every task has a Comments button. In shared projects you can also assign people. See Work with others guide:collaboration . Complete, undo and delete Complete Tick the checkbox, or press Space on a focused row. The task stays in its list, shown as done, for 5 seconds, and longer while your pointer, finger or keyboard focus is on it. Then it collapses. Collapsed tasks share one Completed or \"3 completed\" message with Undo , which stays for 6 seconds. Leaving the list collapses everything at once. The desktop can play a sound on completion. Choose the file, reset it or press Play in Settings, General . On the desktop, Ctrl + K completes the selected task. With a card open, it completes and closes. Delete Choose Delete in the task's menu, or select it and press Delete . With Confirm before deleting on in Settings, General , Vicu asks first. Duplicate and templates Duplicate and Save as template are in the task's menu. See Add tasks guide:capture reuse-a-task . Select several tasks On the desktop Click with Shift or Ctrl to select a range or add single tasks. Ctrl + A selects all. A bar appears to schedule, complete, move, label or delete the whole selection. Assign shows when every task is in a shared project you may edit. Right-click for the same actions with their shortcut hints. On a phone Select tasks, and the selection's menu has the actions for all of them: Copy titles , Duplicate , and Assign... when every task is in a shared project. Move, drag and reorder On the desktop, drag a task to reorder it, onto a project in the sidebar to move it, onto a label to apply it, or onto a section. On a phone, press and hold a task and drag. You can also move a task with the keyboard. See Projects and labels guide:projects task-order . Row density Settings, General, Appearance, Task rows has two choices: - Compact shows one title line per row. This is the default. - Comfortable shows up to three title lines and a line of notes. Swipe actions Swipe right to complete a task and left to open the When panel. In Settings, Gestures you can choose each side from Complete , Choose when , Due today , Due tomorrow , Mark urgent , Move to project , Delete and Nothing . Delete always asks first. ! Swipe actions on Android screen:android-swipe-light.webp Print Press Ctrl + P , or use File, Print , to print the list or task you are looking at. Related - Add tasks guide:capture - Quick add guide:quick-add - Smart lists guide:lists - Projects and labels guide:projects - Work with others guide:collaboration"},{"s":"lists","t":"Smart lists","d":"Inbox, Today, Upcoming, Anytime and Logbook in Vicu: what each one shows, how to hide the ones you do not use, and how to search.","h":[["in-short","In short"],["inbox","Inbox"],["today","Today"],["add-a-task-in-today","Add a task in Today"],["upcoming","Upcoming"],["anytime","Anytime"],["logbook","Logbook"],["other-lists-in-the-sidebar","Other lists in the sidebar"],["hide-the-lists-you-do-not-use","Hide the lists you do not use"],["on-the-desktop","On the desktop"],["on-a-phone","On a phone"],["search","Search"],["on-the-desktop-2","On the desktop"],["on-a-phone-2","On a phone"],["search-filters","Search filters"],["refresh","Refresh"],["related","Related"]],"x":"In short - Inbox holds new tasks until you sort them. - Today shows what is overdue and what is due today. - Upcoming shows everything due from tomorrow on, day by day. - Anytime shows every open task outside the Inbox, with or without a date. - Logbook shows what you have completed. - You can hide any smart list except the Inbox, and still reach it from search or the command palette. Inbox The Inbox is the project you chose at setup. New tasks from quick add, Quick Entry, voice, widgets and the share sheet land here. It is always visible and cannot be hidden. Tasks with a date come first, then the rest in the order you set. On the desktop, the Inbox header has a sort button. You can also drag a task, or on a phone press and hold and drag, to put it where you want. The order syncs. On the desktop, Sort the Inbox in the header starts a guided session that goes through the Inbox one task at a time. See Review and guided organizing guide:review . The Inbox does not appear among your projects in the sidebar. Today Today shows what you can act on now, in sections: - Overdue , oldest first, grouped by project. - Due today , including tasks due earlier today. - Tasks whose time block starts today. - Today's routines, when routines are on. Today follows your local calendar day and rolls over at midnight. When it is empty, Vicu says \"All clear\" and offers the next task. ! Today on the desktop screen:desktop-today.webp On a phone, the date shows under the title. With the planner on, a List and Day switch appears. Add a task in Today A task you add while Today is open is due today. Type a different date to change that. Upcoming Upcoming shows tasks due from tomorrow on, grouped by day. On a phone, the day headers stay at the top as you scroll, and tasks are grouped by project within each day. ! Upcoming on iPhone screen:ios-upcoming-light.webp Anytime Anytime is your backlog: every open task outside the Inbox, whether it has a date or not. It shows every project level. Use it when you want to see everything in one place, or to pull a task forward by giving it a date. ! Anytime on the desktop screen:desktop-anytime.webp Logbook The Logbook lists completed tasks, grouped by the day you completed them, with the time of day. Older tasks load as you scroll or ask for more. On a phone, Settings, General, Logbook sets whether the Logbook keeps tasks and for how many days. Other lists in the sidebar Besides the five smart lists, the sidebar or drawer can show: - Routines , Review and Plan when you turn them on. See Routines guide:routines , Review and guided organizing guide:review and Plan your day guide:plan . - Custom lists you make yourself. See Custom lists guide:custom-lists . - Pro lists such as Flagged and Waiting on, when Pro is on. See Vicu Pro guide:pro . Hide the lists you do not use On the desktop 1. Open Settings, General, Sidebar . 2. Clear the checkbox of each list you want to hide. You can hide Today, Upcoming, Anytime and Logbook, plus Routines and Review while they are on. The Inbox always stays. A hidden list still opens from the command palette. The setting is per computer. On a phone 1. Open Settings, General, Drawer . 2. Turn off the views you do not want. You can hide Today, Upcoming and Anytime while the bottom bar does not hold them , Logbook, Routines, Review, and Ask once it is set up. Reminders, widgets and notifications do not change. The setting is per device. Search On the desktop Press Ctrl + F Cmd + F on a Mac to open Quick find at the top of the sidebar. Results appear as you type. Search looks at titles, notes and finished tasks. ! The command palette screen:desktop-palette.webp \"The command palette finds lists and commands.\" The command palette, Ctrl + Shift + P , jumps to any list, including the ones you hid, and runs any command. On a phone Open Search at the top of a list. Local matches show at once, completed tasks have their own section, and the server is asked in the background. Search filters Under the search title, chips filter by date, status, labels and priority. Filters last while the page is open, and they work with or without text. Use them alone to answer a question like \"high-priority tasks with a label\". Refresh Vicu refreshes when you return to the window, every 5 minutes while it is visible, and after your computer wakes. To refresh by hand on the desktop, press F5 or Ctrl + R . On a phone, pull down at the top of a list. Smart lists roll over to the new day at midnight. Related - How Vicu is organized guide:basics - Work with tasks guide:tasks - Projects and labels guide:projects - Custom lists guide:custom-lists - Plan your day guide:plan"},{"s":"projects","t":"Projects and labels","d":"Organize tasks in Vicu with projects, subprojects, sections, categories and labels. Sort tasks, move projects, archive them and see progress at a glance.","h":[["in-short","In short"],["make-a-project","Make a project"],["on-the-desktop","On the desktop"],["on-a-phone","On a phone"],["the-project-menu","The project menu"],["colors-and-icons","Colors and icons"],["move-and-reorder-projects","Move and reorder projects"],["on-the-desktop-2","On the desktop"],["on-a-phone-2","On a phone"],["categories","Categories"],["sections","Sections"],["labels","Labels"],["task-order","Task order"],["set-the-order","Set the order"],["drag-to-reorder","Drag to reorder"],["move-a-task-from-the-keyboard","Move a task from the keyboard"],["progress-rings-and-counts","Progress rings and counts"],["archive-and-restore","Archive and restore"],["saved-filters","Saved filters"],["related","Related"]],"x":"In short - A project holds tasks , and can hold subprojects and sections. - Categories are optional headers that group your top-level projects. - Labels tag tasks across projects. Each label has its own page. - The project menu has every action: rename, edit, add a subproject, move, sort, share, archive and delete. - Drag projects to nest or reorder them, and drag tasks onto a project or label to move or tag them. - Each project can have its own task order. Make a project On the desktop 1. Click + next to Projects in the sidebar. 2. Type a name in the new row and press Enter . To add a subproject, open the project's menu and choose Add subproject . In Settings, Projects , New project does the same. On a phone Tap + on the drawer's Projects header. To make a subproject, open the project, tap the ⋮ menu and choose Add subproject . The project menu One menu holds the project actions, on every screen that shows a project. - On the desktop, open it from the ... button on a sidebar row, with a right-click, from the project page header, or from the rows in Settings, Projects . With a project focused, Shift + F10 opens it. - On a phone, tap ⋮ on the project screen or on the rows in Settings, Projects . Action What it does --- --- Rename Renames in place. F2 works on a focused project on the desktop. Edit One dialog for name, parent, color and icon Add subproject Makes a project inside this one Move to Puts the project under another Move to category Moves a top-level project under a category header Add category above Makes a new category above this project Set as Inbox Makes this project your Inbox Review Marks it reviewed, and sets its review cadence or leaves it out of review Sort tasks Sets this project's task order Share Shares the project with people. See Work with others guide:collaboration Archive Hides the project without deleting it Delete Deletes it. It asks first when Confirm before deleting is on. On a phone, the menu reads Edit project , Add subproject , Set as Inbox , Mark reviewed , Archive , Delete and Sort tasks . Edit project holds Shared with . A project cannot be put inside the Inbox, and the Inbox cannot be archived. Colors and icons Edit sets a project's color. The icon can be a folder or None . None suits names that start with an emoji, and it syncs. The sidebar, Settings and project pickers then leave the icon out so the name starts where it would. Move and reorder projects On the desktop Drag a project in the sidebar or in Settings, Projects : - Drop on the middle of another project to nest it inside. - Drop on the top third of a row to place it before, and on the bottom third to place it after. - Drop on the PROJECTS header to make it top-level. A move to another parent shows a message with Undo . From the keyboard, focus a project and press Alt + Shift + Arrow Option on a Mac to move it up, down, into the project above, or out one level. On a phone Hold and drag a project within its level. Screen reader users get Move up and Move down actions. Categories If you have many top-level projects, categories group them under named headers. They are optional. Without one, nothing changes. - Choose Add category above in a project's menu, or New category in Settings, Projects , to make one and name it. - Choose Move to category in a project's menu to file it. Or drag a project past a header. - Use the header's menu to rename, move up, move down or delete a category. Deleting has Undo . - Click a header to collapse it. That is remembered per device. Categories sync to all your devices. Two people sharing a project each file it separately. Project pickers show the same headers. Categories apply to top-level projects only. Sections Sections split a project into collapsible groups with headers and counts. Add one from the project menu, or from the strip that shows Add section between sections. A group of one has no header. On a phone, collapsed sections are remembered while the app runs. Labels A label is a colored tag with its own page. Chip text always stays readable against the label's color. - Put labels on a task in its card, or type @errand in quick add. See Quick add guide:quick-add . - Open a label from Tags in the sidebar, or Labels in the phone drawer, to see all its tasks. - Drag a task onto a label in the sidebar to apply it. - On a phone, create labels from the picker. Tapping Create when a search finds nothing makes the label. Drag labels in the drawer to reorder them; the order stays on that device. Task order A project's tasks are sorted in the project's order. The default is Due date, then manual : dated tasks first by date, then the rest in the order you drag them. The choices are Due date, then manual , Due date, then priority , Manual , Priority , Title , Newest first and Oldest first . Set the order - For one project, open its menu and choose Sort tasks . Default makes it follow its parent project again. The Inbox has a sort button in its header. - For all projects, open Settings, Projects, Display and use Sort project tasks by . It shows how many projects have their own order, with Reset . A subproject follows its parent's order unless it has one of its own. The order syncs, and phone widgets follow it. Drag to reorder What you can drag depends on the order: Order What you can drag --- --- Manual Every task Due date, then manual Tasks without a date, among themselves Any other order Nothing. A message says why. Move a task from the keyboard Press Alt + Shift + Up or Down on a focused task Option + Shift on a Mac , or choose Move up or Move down in its menu. The same rule applies as for dragging. On a phone, Move up and Move down are screen reader actions. Progress rings and counts Each project row can show a small ring of done and open tasks. - Show project progress in the sidebar is on by default. - Show task count in the sidebar adds the number of open tasks and is off until you turn it on. With both on, a row shows the ring and then the count. Both are in Settings, Projects, Display , per device. Hidden tasks, such as the ones Vicu uses for routines, never count. Archive and restore Archiving hides a project but keeps it. Archived projects collect in an Archived group in the sidebar. Choose Restore on one, or Restore project on its page, to bring it back. An archived project's menu also has Delete . Saved filters If you used saved filters in Vikunja, Vicu turns them into custom lists. See Custom lists guide:custom-lists vikunja-saved-filters . Related - How Vicu is organized guide:basics - Smart lists guide:lists - Custom lists guide:custom-lists - Work with others guide:collaboration - Review and guided organizing guide:review"},{"s":"custom-lists","t":"Custom lists","d":"Make your own lists in Vicu from conditions such as project, label, due date and priority, add a rule in Vikunja filter syntax, and keep them in sync across devices.","h":[["in-short","In short"],["make-a-list","Make a list"],["on-the-desktop","On the desktop"],["on-a-phone","On a phone"],["the-conditions","The conditions"],["due-date-windows","Due date windows"],["shared-work","Shared work"],["sort-and-then-by","Sort and Then by"],["rules","Rules"],["icons","Icons"],["vikunja-saved-filters","Vikunja saved filters"],["sync-between-devices","Sync between devices"],["more-conditions-with-pro","More conditions with Pro"],["related","Related"]],"x":"In short - A custom list is a set of conditions , not a folder. Tasks appear in it when they match. - Conditions cover projects, labels, priority, due dates, completion, changes, and shared work. - A rule lets you write any condition in Vikunja's filter syntax. - Lists sync. They live on your server, so every device shows the same ones. - Quick View , phone widgets and the phone's bottom bar can show a custom list. - Vikunja's saved filters are imported as custom lists. Make a list On the desktop 1. Click the + button next to your custom lists in the sidebar. 2. Type a Name and pick an Icon . 3. Set the conditions you want. 4. Save. To change a list later, open its menu in the sidebar and choose edit. ! The custom list editor screen:desktop-list-editor.webp On a phone Open the drawer and use the + on the custom lists section, then fill in the same fields. You can show a custom list in the bottom bar or on a widget. The conditions A task has to match all the conditions you set. Leave a condition on \"any\" to ignore it. Condition What it does --- --- Projects Only tasks in the projects you pick Labels Only tasks with the labels you pick Priority Only tasks with the priorities you tick: None, Low, Medium, High or Urgent Due Date A window, described below Changed Tasks changed today, or in the last 3, 7 or 30 days Include completed tasks Adds done tasks to the list Include tasks due today from all projects Pulls today's tasks in from every project Add tasks to The project that new tasks from this list go to, and the Inbox by default Due date windows The Due Date choices are All , Overdue , Today , This Week , This Month , Has Due Date and No Due Date . For Today, This Week and This Month, Include overdue tasks is on by default, so a task that slipped past still shows up. Turn it off to see only what is due in the window. The window follows your local calendar day, and the list shows the right tasks at midnight without a refresh. Shared work When you share projects with others, the editor shows a Shared projects section: - Assigned : tasks assigned To me , To someone else or To nobody . - Comments : Unread comments or Has comments . - Only tasks in shared projects . Assigned and unread comments match tasks in shared projects only. Has comments matches every project. A task you read in an unread list keeps its place until you leave the list. The section appears only while something is shared, or when the list already uses one of these conditions. See Work with others guide:collaboration . Sort and Then by Sort by sets the main order, with Order for ascending or descending. The options are Due date , Start date , End date , Created , Updated , Completed , Priority , Progress , Title , Done , Order created and Position . The order names follow the field, such as \"Soonest first\" and \"A to Z\". Tasks without the date come last in both directions. Then by adds a second sort for tasks that tie on the first. Leave it as None if you do not need one. Rules A rule is a condition written in Vikunja's filter syntax. It is checked in addition to the conditions above. For example: done = false && priority 2 The Rule box shows label and project names instead of numbers, and suggests fields, operators and values as you type. Save stays off until the rule reads correctly. Tick Include tasks without a value if tasks with an empty field should match too. Quick View and the assistant follow the rule as well. Icons The Icon picker shows the icons themselves. On the desktop use the arrow keys, Home and End to move through them. None leaves the icon out of the sidebar, so the name starts where the icon would, which suits a name that begins with an emoji. Vikunja saved filters In Vikunja, saved filters used to show up in Vicu as empty projects. Vicu now imports every saved filter it can read as a custom list. The import happens the first time a device syncs its custom lists. An import is a copy: the list says so once, and later changes in Vikunja do not change it. Two devices never import the same filter twice. To bring in filters you made later, or to import them again: - On the desktop, open Settings, Projects, Saved filters and choose Import saved filters . - On a phone, open Settings, Projects, Custom Lists and tap Import saved filters . Sync between devices Custom lists are stored on your server in a hidden task, so every device shows the same lists after it syncs. Vicu syncs them when it starts, when you return to the window and every 5 minutes. A deleted list is cleaned up after 90 days. If you open a list on an older version of Vicu that does not know a newer condition, it ignores that condition and shows more tasks. Update all your devices to stay in step. On the desktop, the sync panel lists custom-list changes that have not reached the server, with Sync now and Discard . See Sync and offline guide:sync . More conditions with Pro Vicu Pro adds conditions such as Available now, Flagged, estimates, project status and promises, plus grouping and a table view. See Power tools guide:pro/power . Related - Smart lists guide:lists - Projects and labels guide:projects - Work with others guide:collaboration - Power tools guide:pro/power - Sync and offline guide:sync"},{"s":"routines","t":"Routines","d":"Routines track what you do regularly, like taking vitamins or watering plants, with times of day, reminders, history and sharing.","h":[["in-short","In short"],["make-a-routine","Make a routine"],["schedule","Schedule"],["times-and-reminders","Times and reminders"],["tick-skip-and-undo","Tick, skip and undo"],["routines-on-today","Routines on Today"],["history-and-export","History and export"],["reminders","Reminders"],["share-a-routine","Share a routine"],["who-can-do-what","Who can do what"],["archive-a-routine","Archive a routine"],["desktop-and-phone","Desktop and phone"],["related","Related"]],"x":"In short - A routine is not a repeating task. It has a name, a schedule and up to five times a day. A missed day is just \"Not logged\": routines never turn overdue and never pile up. - Open Routines from the sidebar desktop or the drawer phone . Today's routines also show on Today. - Tick, skip or undo a routine at each of its times. Vicu keeps the history and shows how often you logged it. - Share a routine with someone you already share a project with. Whoever does it first ticks it for both. - Routines are optional. Turn them off in Settings and the sidebar entry, the Today section and the reminders go. Your routines and history are kept. Make a routine Schedule Choice What it does --- --- On a schedule Pick the weekdays, or Daily for every day. Set Repeat to every week, every 2, 3 or 4 weeks. After completion Show again a number of days after you complete it. Use this for things like \"every 2 weeks since the last time\". Times and reminders A routine has one to five times a day. Each time has: - A time of day - Remind me , which sends a notification at that time - Follow up desktop , a second reminder 15 minutes, 30 minutes or 1 hour later. Off by default. Use Add time for more times. For example, \"Feed the cat\" at 08:00 and 18:00. A routine's colour is stored with the routine. Colours chosen on the desktop appear as a swatch on the phone. A routine's colour is stored with the routine. Colours chosen on the desktop appear as a swatch on the phone. Tick, skip and undo Each occurrence has a status: done, skipped or not logged. - Tick the circle to mark a time done. Tick again to undo. - Skip leaves it out without counting as done. On the desktop, Skip shows when you hover the row. Undo takes a skip back. - A time you did nothing about becomes Not logged . It is not overdue and nothing nags. On the phone, the routines widget lets you log a routine without opening the app. On an iPhone, reminders for a routine have Complete and Skip actions. Routines on Today Today shows a Routines section with the routines still open for the day. Finished and skipped ones leave. The section folds from its header. Today shows one row per routine, at its next time, with progress like \"1/3\" when it has more than one time. To keep Today short, only routines due within two hours or carried over from earlier show at once. The rest wait behind one line such as \"2 later today\", or \"Show 3 more\", which opens until you leave the view. Set how many show: - Desktop: Settings, General, Routines , then Routines shown on Today : 1, 2 the default , 3, 5 or All. - Phone: Settings, General, Routines . The same place has Enable routines and Show routines in Today . Turning off Show routines in Today keeps the Routines list but removes the section from Today. This is per device. History and export On the desktop, click a routine's name or its History button. It shows the share of logged times you completed \"Logged adherence\" , the number of completions and every check-in with its date, time and status. Vicu keeps the last 400 days of detail with the routine. Older history is stored in hidden \"Vicu routine archive\" tasks on your server, and is still included in the history and in the export. Do not delete those tasks in the Vikunja web app. - Desktop: the Export button at the top of Routines saves all routines' history as a CSV file. - Phone: use Copy history as CSV in the Routines menu. Reminders Reminders follow each time's Remind me switch and the usual notification settings. See Settings guide:settings for the notification options. - On the phone, Pause all reminders in the Routines menu silences every routine at once. - On an iPhone, routine reminders have Complete and Skip actions. - On Android, Vicu checks the routine on your server before a reminder rings, so a time already done elsewhere stays quiet. Share a routine You can share a routine with another person so that either of you ticking it counts for both, on every device. A routine is shared through a project you already share with that person. Vicu never creates or shares a project for you. The routine moves into that project. To stop, choose Only you under Share with ; the routine goes back to your Inbox. Who can do what Owner Member who can edit Member who can only view --- --- --- --- Tick, skip and undo Yes Yes No Edit Yes Yes No Turn their own reminders off, hide it Yes Yes Hide only Stop sharing, archive, delete Yes No No - A finished time says who did it, such as \"Done by Maya\" or \"Done by you\". If you both tick at the same moment, both count. - A member can turn off their own reminders for a shared routine, or hide it for themselves. Both follow them to their other devices. Hidden routines are listed under Hidden shared routines in Settings, General, Routines , where you can show them again. - Sharing needs a connection. - Deleting or archiving a project that holds a shared routine warns you first, even when you have turned off confirmations for deleting. Deleting the project deletes the routine and its history for everyone. Archive a routine Archive a routine you no longer do but want to keep. On the desktop, hover its row and choose the archive button. Archived routines wait under Archived at the bottom of the list, where the owner can Restore or delete one. Only a routine's owner archives or deletes it. Desktop and phone Desktop Phone --- --- --- Open Routines in the sidebar Routines in the drawer, and a widget Settings Settings, General, Routines Settings, General, Routines CSV Export button Copy history as CSV Reminders Notification at the time, with an optional follow up Pause all; Complete and Skip actions on iPhone Related - Plan your day guide:plan - Collaboration and sharing guide:collaboration - Basics guide:basics - Settings guide:settings"},{"s":"review","t":"Review and guided organizing","d":"Keep projects current with project review, sort the Inbox and overdue tasks one decision at a time, and run a weekly review.","h":[["in-short","In short"],["project-review","Project review"],["open-the-review-list","Open the Review list"],["mark-a-project-reviewed","Mark a project reviewed"],["set-the-cadence","Set the cadence"],["review-settings","Review settings"],["guided-organizing","Guided organizing"],["the-four-sessions","The four sessions"],["a-card","A card"],["keys","Keys"],["review-projects-session","Review projects session"],["the-weekly-review","The weekly review"],["with-the-assistant","With the assistant"],["desktop-and-phone","Desktop and phone"],["related","Related"]],"x":"In short - Project review brings each project back on a schedule you set. You look at it, then choose Mark reviewed . - Guided organizing walks you through tasks one decision at a time: the Inbox, overdue tasks, tasks that have waited a long time, and projects due for review. - The weekly review is a checklist in the usual order: Inbox, what is on your mind, last week, overdue, next two weeks, projects, long-waiting tasks. - You decide everything. Sessions work without the assistant. With the assistant set up, it suggests a choice for each card, and you still decide. - Undo all at the end of a session puts back what it changed. It never brings back a deleted task. Project review Project review makes sure no project goes stale. Each project has a review cadence, and Vicu tells you when one is due. Open the Review list Open Review from the sidebar or drawer. It has two tabs: Due , the projects whose review time has come, and All tracked . A project that was never reviewed is \"Not reviewed yet\". A due project shows as due. On the desktop, click a project to look at its tasks in place, and press R to mark it reviewed. Start review at the top begins the guided project review below. On the phone, use Mark reviewed on a project's row. Mark a project reviewed - Desktop: the project's menu, Review , Mark reviewed , or R in the Review list. - Phone: Mark reviewed on the project's row in Review, or in the project's menu. It has Undo. Vicu records the review as a short line at the end of the project's description it starts with \"Vicu review\" and holds the date and cadence . The description is part of the project in Vikunja, so the web app and your other devices see the same status. Leave that line alone. Set the cadence The default is every 14 days. Change it for one project: On the desktop, open the project's menu, then Review , then Review cadence . On the phone the default cadence is set under Settings, Projects, Review . On the desktop, leave a project out with Exclude from review and put it back with Include in review . On the phone, an Excluded from review list in Settings, Projects, Review lets you include a project again. Review settings Settings, Projects, Review has: - Enable project review tracking - Default review cadence days - Exclude Inbox from review list - The list of excluded projects, each with Include again The phone has review settings in the same place, Settings, Projects, Review . Guided organizing A guided session shows one card at a time with how far along you are, such as \"4 of 14\". The tasks are picked when the session starts. A task finished or deleted elsewhere is passed over. Tasks in archived projects, and in projects shared with you for viewing only, never come up. The four sessions Session What it shows Start it from --- --- --- Sort the Inbox Every open task in the Inbox, in the Inbox's order The Inbox header desktop or top bar phone , the weekly review, Ask Sort out overdue Every open task past its date, oldest first The command palette desktop , the weekly review, Ask Waiting a long time Tasks outside the Inbox without a date or repeat that nobody touched for 60 days or more, up to 30 The command palette desktop , the weekly review Review projects Each project due for review, one page each Start review in Review, the weekly review, Ask On the desktop, press Ctrl + Shift + P Cmd on a Mac and type the session's name. A card A card shows the task's title, project, date and labels, and how long ago it was added. You can: - Give it a date: Today , Tomorrow , This weekend , Next week , a date you pick, or No date . - Move it to another project. - Change the title on the card. - Complete or Delete it. Delete asks first when Confirm before deleting is on. - Skip it. Next saves the card's choices together and shows the next card. If you changed nothing, Next is a Skip. Back shows the previous card as the task is now. Changes made offline wait in the queue like any other change. At the end a summary says what happened, for example \"Sorted 12: 5 scheduled, 4 moved, 2 completed, 1 deleted\". Undo all puts back every change, except deletes. Keys Key Does --- --- Enter Next Left Back S Skip 1 to 4 Today, Tomorrow, This weekend, Next week D Pick a date 0 No date P Project E Title C Complete Delete Delete Esc Leave the session Review projects session Each project due for review gets one page: 1. Its name, when it was last reviewed, and a health line such as \"7 open · 4 untouched for 30+ days · 1 overdue · last done 6 days ago\". 2. Still working on it? Keep it, or Archive it. An archived project is not marked reviewed. The Inbox cannot be archived. 3. Untouched for 30+ days: tasks nobody changed in a month, oldest first, each with the card choices. 4. Next step: the first open task in the project's order, or the one the assistant suggests, with quick dates. Or name a new task and add it to the project. 5. Mark reviewed records the review and goes on. Skip goes on without it. On the desktop R marks reviewed, S skips and A archives. A subproject is reviewed as a project of its own. Undo all at the end also takes back the reviews, the archives and the tasks you added. The weekly review Open Review , then Weekly review . On the desktop, \"Weekly review\" is also in the command palette. It is a checklist: 1. Sort the Inbox 2. Anything on your mind? Starts a brain dump when smart dictation is set up. Otherwise you add tasks to the Inbox right there. 3. Look back: what you finished in the last 7 days 4. Overdue 5. Look ahead: what is due in the next two weeks 6. Review projects , while project review is on 7. Waiting a long time Next opens the first step not done yet, and you can open any step. A step is done when its session ends or you choose Mark done . A step with nothing in it is done from the start. Finish records the day of the review and syncs it, so your other devices know. A week later the assistant offers the weekly review again. With Pro guide:pro/power on, the weekly review adds steps for verdicts, recap, escalations, the portfolio and the value you delivered. With Pro guide:pro/power on, the weekly review adds steps for verdicts, recap, escalations, the portfolio and the value you delivered. With the assistant When the assistant is set up see The assistant guide:assistant , each card comes with a suggestion. The choices it would make are filled in with a dashed outline, and its reason is under the title. Change or clear any of it. Nothing is saved until you choose Next . - When it thinks a task is finished or outdated, Complete or Delete is marked \"Suggested\". It is never done for you. - Tasks go to the model 20 at a time, and the next 20 are asked a few cards ahead. You can decide a card before its suggestion arrives. - If a request fails, the session says so once and goes on without suggestions. - There is no separate setting. Suggestions use the model you chose for Ask and what you agreed to share for Ask. What is sent: the names of your projects, labels and places, today's date, and the session's tasks title, project, dates, labels, priority, when they were added and changed, and the first 500 characters of their notes . Text from shared projects is treated as data, never as instructions. In Ask, an empty conversation begins with what needs a decision the Inbox, overdue tasks, projects due for review, the weekly review and each opens its session. Desktop and phone Both apps use the same rules and pick the same tasks in the same order. Guided sessions arrived on the desktop first. Keys exist on the desktop only. On the phone, use the buttons on each card, and start sessions from the Inbox's top bar, the Review screen or Ask. Related - Projects guide:projects - Plan your day guide:plan - The assistant guide:assistant - Shortcuts guide:shortcuts"},{"s":"collaboration","t":"Sharing, assignees and comments","d":"Share a project with other people on your Vikunja server, assign tasks, write comments with mentions and files, and build lists for shared work.","h":[["in-short","In short"],["share-a-project","Share a project"],["assign-a-task","Assign a task"],["assign-several-tasks-at-once","Assign several tasks at once"],["comments","Comments"],["open-the-comments","Open the comments"],["mention-someone","Mention someone"],["add-files","Add files"],["notify-me","Notify me"],["lists-for-shared-work","Lists for shared work"],["routines-you-share","Routines you share"],["details","Details"],["related","Related"]],"x":"In short - Share a whole project , not single tasks. Pick a person and choose Can view , Can edit or Admin . Only an admin of the project can share it. - Assign tasks to the people in a shared project. They get Vikunja's own notification. - Every task has comments , even in a private project, where they work as notes to yourself. - What involves other people shows only where it matters. On a project only you can see, you do not see assignees, mentions, Notify me or unread marks. - Comments and assignments work offline. They wait in the queue and send in order. - You and the people you share with all need an account on the same Vikunja server . Share a project Rules: - Only an admin of the project sees Share . - Sharing a project also gives access to its subprojects. - Names and emails only match people who made themselves findable in Vikunja. - A removal has Undo on the desktop, in the dialog. - Teams are managed in Vikunja. Their members show in the list but you cannot change the team from Vicu. A shared project gets a people icon in the sidebar. Assign a task In a shared project, a task has an Assign chip right after the project. Pick people from the list. Rows show who is assigned, you first. Vikunja notifies the person. - People who can only view are shown as \"Can view only\" and cannot be picked. - Assign and unassign work offline and send when the connection is back. Assign several tasks at once Select two or more tasks that all sit in shared projects you can edit. Assign appears in the selection bar and in the right-click menu on the desktop, and as Assign... in the selection's menu on the phone. A person is ticked when everyone has them, partly ticked when only some do. Picking someone adds them where they are missing. Picking a ticked person removes them from all. Comments Every task has Comments . The count shows on the row, with a dot when there are comments you have not read. Open the comments - Desktop: click the count, use the Comments button on the open task, or press Ctrl + Shift + C Cmd on a Mac . The pane opens beside the list and stays open as you move between tasks until you close it. - Phone: open the task and tap Comments . The pane says who can read the thread. Post with Ctrl + Enter on the desktop. You can edit and delete your own comments hold a comment on the phone . Someone who can only view the project can read but not write. Opening a thread marks it read on all your devices. Mention someone Type @ and pick a name. They get a notification. Add files Attach files to a comment with the paperclip, by pasting or by dropping them desktop . On the phone, use attach, or paste an image. - Images show in the thread at a preview size and open in the viewer. Other files show as a row with Open , Save and, on the phone, Share. - Files upload when you add them, so you need a connection and a saved task. - Editing a comment keeps its files. Deleting a comment leaves its files on the task, and the question says so. - A file deleted from the task reads \"No longer available\". Notify me The Notify me button in the comments pane follows the task, so you are told about new comments. It reads \"Notifying you\" once on. It is only offered when you signed in with a password or single sign-on, not with an API token. If you sign in with an API token , Vikunja can refuse some actions without a clear reason. When a token lacks permission for task comments, task assignees or project sharing, Vicu says which one is missing. Create a new token in Vikunja with those permissions, or sign in with your password. A change the token may not send goes to the list of changes that could not be sent, and the others carry on. If you sign in with an API token , Vikunja can refuse some actions without a clear reason. When a token lacks permission for task comments, task assignees or project sharing, Vicu says which one is missing. Create a new token in Vikunja with those permissions, or sign in with your password. A change the token may not send goes to the list of changes that could not be sent, and the others carry on. Lists for shared work A custom list guide:custom-lists can look at collaboration. In the list editor, the Shared projects section appears when something is shared or the list already uses one of these: - Assigned: to me, to someone else, or to nobody - Comments: unread or has comments. \"Has comments\" covers every project. \"Unread\" covers shared projects. - Only tasks in shared projects - Changed: today, or the last 3, 7 or 30 days A task you read in an Unread list keeps its place until you leave the list, so it does not vanish while you read. Quick View on the desktop follows the same conditions. Routines you share A routine can be shared through a shared project. See Routines guide:routines . Details - Trusted text. The assistant treats titles, notes and names from shared projects as data and never as instructions. - One account, one set of data. Signing in as another person on the same install clears the first person's custom lists and settings, so two people never mix them. Related - Projects guide:projects - Custom lists guide:custom-lists - Routines guide:routines - Sync and offline guide:sync"},{"s":"assistant","t":"The assistant","d":"Set up Vicu's optional AI assistant with your own model, then use smart dictation, brain dump, Ask and Talk, and read what is sent and what stays on your device.","h":[["in-short","In short"],["what-it-does-and-does-not-do","What it does and does not do"],["set-it-up","Set it up"],["providers","Providers"],["models","Models"],["consent","Consent"],["smart-dictation","Smart dictation"],["start-smart-dictation","Start smart dictation"],["quick-capture-and-smart-dictation","Quick capture and smart dictation"],["brain-dump","Brain dump"],["make-tasks-from-text","Make tasks from text"],["break-a-task-into-steps","Break a task into steps"],["ask","Ask"],["changes-come-as-cards","Changes come as cards"],["conversations","Conversations"],["talk","Talk"],["speech-on-this-device","Speech on this device"],["privacy-what-is-sent-and-what-stays","Privacy: what is sent and what stays"],["assistant-help-in-pro","Assistant help in Pro"],["desktop-and-phone","Desktop and phone"],["related","Related"]],"x":"In short - It is optional and off. Nothing is sent anywhere until you pick a provider, save a key and agree. - You bring the model. Vicu has no AI service of its own. You choose a provider OpenAI, Anthropic, Google Gemini, OpenRouter, Ollama, Open WebUI or any compatible server and use your own key. - It advises, you decide. Tasks appear as draft cards you add or decline. Changes Ask proposes wait for your approval, and each has Undo. - Your key stays on the device , encrypted, and is never synced. - Voice has two paths. Quick capture turns a spoken sentence into one task and never uses the model. Smart dictation uses the model for several tasks at a time. - The phone and the desktop work the same way. Where the entry points differ, this page says so. What it does and does not do The assistant can: - Turn speech or pasted text into one or many tasks smart dictation guide:assistant smart-dictation , Make tasks from text guide:assistant make-tasks-from-text . - Turn a long ramble into a plan with projects brain dump guide:assistant brain-dump . - Answer questions about your tasks and propose changes Ask guide:assistant ask , also by voice Talk guide:assistant talk . - Suggest steps for a task Break into steps guide:assistant break-a-task-into-steps . - Suggest a decision on each card in a guided session guide:review and a day in Plan my day guide:plan . It does not: - Change, complete or delete anything by itself. - Reach the internet, open links or look at images. - Send audio anywhere. Only the recognized words are sent. - Treat text from shared projects titles, notes, other people's names as instructions. That text is only data. - Dictate inside the Quick Entry window. Set it up Providers OpenAI, Anthropic, Google Gemini, OpenRouter, Ollama, Open WebUI, or Other for any server that speaks the OpenAI chat format. For Ollama, Open WebUI and Other you can change the address. Ollama's default is http://localhost:11434/v1 . If the address needs a version part such as /v1 and lacks it, Vicu hints at that. Each provider remembers its own address, models and key. Ollama, Open WebUI and Other keep up to five saved addresses, each with its own models and key Add address . Models Use one model for everything, or turn on Use a different model for Ask to give Ask and Talk their own. A model tests itself once chosen and the key is saved. The test checks that it can give a structured answer needed for dictation and call a tool needed for Ask . Test connection runs it again. If a model cannot run a feature, that feature's switch stays off and says why. Choose another model, or start your local server and turn it on again. Consent The first time something would be sent to a provider that is not on your own network, Vicu shows what each feature sends and asks you to choose Agree . A server on your own computer or local network does not ask. An http:// address that points outside your network gets a warning, because your key and text would travel unencrypted. Smart dictation Smart dictation holds several tasks at once. Say \"Buy milk, call the plumber on Friday about the leak, and book the dentist for next month\". When you pause for about a second, your words go to the model and come back as draft cards , one per task, with title, notes, date and time, project, labels, priority, repeat, reminder and steps. Keep talking. Each later pause is sent as a change to the cards \"make the first one Friday\", \"drop the dry cleaning\" or as more tasks \"and call mom tomorrow\" . If you start talking before the cards come back, that request stops and your words go out together after the next pause. Nothing is added until you choose Add , and then there is one Undo for the lot. - Fix a card by hand or remove it. A dashed outline marks a value the model guessed. - Dates are checked by Vicu's own quick-add guide:quick-add rules, so \"Friday\" means what it means when you type it. - A new task goes to the project of the list you are in, else your Inbox. - If a request fails, the transcript is kept, also across a restart, so you can retry or add it as one plain task. - The microphone turns off after a minute of silence. This is the open microphone: it keeps listening between pauses, so you can talk naturally. Start smart dictation - Tap the voice shortcut the one you set in Settings, Keyboard Shortcuts, Voice input . Tapping, not holding, starts smart dictation when it is ready. - Hold the + of a list and slide up to the sparkle. A chip says \"Slide up to lock: smart dictation\". The words so far carry over. - Choose Dictate tasks in the command palette Ctrl + Shift + P , Cmd on a Mac . - The panel shows the words being heard. Done turns the microphone off. The microphone button turns it on again. - Tap the voice shortcut the one you set in Settings, Keyboard Shortcuts, Voice input . Tapping, not holding, starts smart dictation when it is ready. - Hold the + of a list and slide up to the sparkle. A chip says \"Slide up to lock: smart dictation\". The words so far carry over. - Choose Dictate tasks in the command palette Ctrl + Shift + P , Cmd on a Mac . - The panel shows the words being heard. Done turns the microphone off. The microphone button turns it on again. - Hold + and slide up to the sparkle. - Hold the app icon and choose Dictate . It opens listening in the smart dictation sheet for your Inbox when the assistant is ready. - On an iPhone you can also ask Siri to add a task, or use Shortcuts. See Quick capture guide:capture . - Hold + and slide up to the sparkle. - Hold the app icon and choose Dictate . It opens listening in the smart dictation sheet for your Inbox when the assistant is ready. - On an iPhone you can also ask Siri to add a task, or use Shortcuts. See Quick capture guide:capture . Quick capture and smart dictation Quick capture Smart dictation --- --- --- Start Hold + , or hold the voice shortcut, or Say a task in the palette Slide up from a held + , tap the voice shortcut, Dictate tasks Result One task, read like quick add Draft cards for you to review Uses the model Never Yes Needs the assistant No Yes Quick capture adds the task at once with Undo, to the list on screen. See Capture guide:capture for how to hold the + and the voice settings. Brain dump For a long ramble, turn on Brain dump in the smart dictation sheet, or choose Brain dump in the command palette. It is offered until something has been sent, and the sheet remembers it on this device. Then talk or type as long as you like. What you say is added to the end of the dump, which you can edit. Nothing is sent until you choose Organize . The microphone stays on for five minutes without speech, and stops when the dump reaches 20,000 characters. The answer is a plan: - Tasks are grouped under the project they go in, with notes, steps, priority, dates and labels. - Where none of your projects fit, it suggests new projects and subprojects , marked New. Rename a new project in place, or remove it. Its tasks move to where it would have gone. A suggestion matching a project you already have uses yours. - Anything you say or type afterwards waits in a field until you choose Update , which sends it as a change \"put the shed tasks in Home\", \"add: book the dentist\" . Add Add all on the phone creates the new projects first, then the tasks. New projects need a connection. If one cannot be created, nothing is added, and the projects already made are removed again. One message says what was added, such as \"Added 2 projects and 14 tasks\", with Undo. If organizing fails, the dump is kept, also across a restart: Retry sends it again, Edit goes back to it. Make tasks from text Turn pasted notes or an email into task cards. - Desktop: choose Make tasks from text in the command palette. Press Ctrl + Enter to make the tasks. - Phone: share the text to Vicu and choose Make tasks . Break a task into steps The assistant proposes subtasks for a task, and you check them before they are added. - Desktop: right-click the task and choose Break into steps . - Phone: use the task's menu. Ask Ask answers questions about your tasks, briefly, the answer first: \"What is overdue in Work?\" or \"What did I finish last week?\" It looks things up with a few read-only tools. Tasks it mentions show as rows you can open or tick. The list you are in is its starting point. Press the Ask button the sparkle next to Search at the top of the sidebar, press Ctrl + J Cmd + J on a Mac , or choose Ask Vicu in the command palette. Press Enter to send. Press the Ask button the sparkle next to Search at the top of the sidebar, press Ctrl + J Cmd + J on a Mac , or choose Ask Vicu in the command palette. Press Enter to send. Tap the sparkle next to Search on any list. While Ask works there is a Stop button, and after a failure a Retry. Tap the sparkle next to Search on any list. While Ask works there is a Stop button, and after a failure a Retry. Changes come as cards When you ask Ask to add, change or complete tasks, it shows a card with each task and its new values. You apply or decline it. After applying, one message offers Undo. - Notes are only ever added to, never replaced. - A change to more than 20 tasks asks twice. - You can change a due date on a proposal before applying it. Conversations When the last conversation has been idle for more than 30 minutes, Ask opens a new one. The older ones stay in the conversation list; the last five are kept on this device. An empty conversation starts with what needs a decision the Inbox, overdue tasks, projects due for review, the weekly review , each opening its session, and a few questions for the list you are in. Tapping a question sends it as written. Talk Talk is Ask by voice. Turn on the microphone in Ask and talk. Each pause sends what you said, and the answer, usually one sentence, is read aloud with the system voice. - The microphone waits while the voice speaks and listens again afterwards. Press the microphone button to stop the voice and talk at once. - Start talking while an answer is coming and it is dropped. Your earlier and new words go out together. - While a card waits, answer \"yes\" or \"no\" out loud. Anything else is sent as your next message. - The microphone turns off if you press it again, close Ask, or stay silent for a minute. - On the phone, with headphones, it keeps listening while it speaks. Speech on this device Voice normally uses the system's speech recognizer. Under Settings, Assistant, Hearing you can download a speech model so speech is recognized on the device, offline, with your project and label names as hints. Audio never leaves the device. Only the recognized text goes to the model. - Desktop: models range from about 60 MB to about 870 MB. Larger ones are more accurate. The one marked Suggested is the recommended one. Choose Download , Pause , Resume or Delete , and use the radio button to pick which is In use . There is also a Language choice. - Phone: a Parakeet model is recommended for phones, since the larger Whisper models are slow. Downloads happen on Wi-Fi unless you allow mobile data. On Android a 64-bit processor is needed. - Without a downloaded model, the system recognizer is used. On macOS that is Apple's, on Windows the built-in offline recognizer, on Linux there is none. Privacy: what is sent and what stays Sent to your provider: - Dictation and brain dump: the words never audio , the names of your projects, labels and places, and the date and time. - Ask: your messages and what its tools look up. - Guided sessions: the same names and date, plus the session's tasks title, project, dates, labels, priority, when they were added and changed, and the first 500 characters of notes . - Plan my day: the same names and date, the candidate tasks, your free time and your routines' times without their names. - Calendar events: never. Only free time ranges. - Pro's data: only when Assistant help in Pro is on. See Vicu Pro guide:pro . Kept on this device only: - The last five conversations and any transcript kept after a failed request. They are deleted when you sign out, or when you choose Delete conversations under Settings, Assistant, Privacy . - The assistant's settings and your key. They belong to the device, not to your Vikunja account. They are not synced and stay when you sign out. The key is stored encrypted on the device and never written to a log. Assistant help in Pro Pro guide:pro has its own switch, Assistant help in Pro , off by default. With it on, Ask can look up promises, people and the portfolio, an import can convert plain notes, and a meeting can draft promises from what was said. With it off, the assistant sees your tasks as before and none of Pro's records. Desktop and phone Desktop Phone --- --- --- Settings Settings, Assistant Settings, Assistant Smart dictation Tap the voice shortcut, slide up from + , Dictate tasks Slide up from + , app icon Dictate Ask Button next to Search, Ctrl + J , Ask Vicu Sparkle next to Search on any list Make tasks from text Command palette Share text to Vicu Break into steps Right-click a task Task menu Speech models Whisper Turbo suggested Parakeet suggested Related - Capture guide:capture - Quick add guide:quick-add - Review and guided organizing guide:review - Plan your day guide:plan - Vicu Pro guide:pro"},{"s":"desktop","t":"Vicu on the desktop","d":"What the desktop app adds on Windows, macOS and Linux: Quick Entry, Quick View, the command palette, Quick find, the tray, startup options and browser or Obsidian links.","h":[["in-short","In short"],["quick-entry","Quick Entry"],["turn-it-on","Turn it on"],["use-it","Use it"],["quick-view","Quick View"],["global-shortcuts-and-wayland","Global shortcuts and Wayland"],["the-tray-startup-and-the-window","The tray, startup and the window"],["how-the-window-looks-on-each-system","How the window looks on each system"],["the-command-palette","The command palette"],["quick-find","Quick find"],["refresh","Refresh"],["right-click-text-menu","Right-click text menu"],["browser-and-obsidian-links","Browser and Obsidian links"],["links-into-vicu","Links into Vicu"],["related","Related"]],"x":"In short - Quick Entry and Quick View are off until you turn them on in Settings, Quick Entry / View . Each has its own shortcut that works in any app. - The command palette opens with Ctrl + Shift + P Cmd on a Mac . It jumps to any list, project, label or task and runs actions. - Quick find is the search box at the top of the sidebar. Ctrl + F puts the cursor there. - The tray icon appears while Quick Entry or Quick View is on. Closing the window then hides Vicu instead of quitting it. - F5 refreshes the page you are on from your server. - Browser and Obsidian links attach the page or note you were looking at to a new task. Quick Entry Quick Entry is a small box that opens over whatever you are doing. Type a task, press Enter , and it is gone. It uses quick add guide:quick-add , so dates, labels, projects and priority typed in the title are read as you type. ! Quick Entry over another app screen:desktop-quick-entry.webp \"Quick Entry\" Turn it on Use it - Type the task and press Enter . Esc closes the box without saving. - Press Tab to add a description under the title. - Hold the project cycle modifier Ctrl by default and press Left or Right to switch to another project. The box says \"saving to\" with the project's name. Add the projects to cycle through under Secondary Projects , and pick the modifier under Project Cycle Modifier . - Press Ctrl + L Cmd + L on a Mac to link the page or note you were looking at. See Browser and Obsidian links guide:desktop browser-and-obsidian-links . - It works offline. The task waits in the offline queue guide:sync the-offline-queue and goes to the server when you are back. Quick View Quick View is a short list in a floating window. Use it to check what is next and tick things off without leaving the app you are in. ! Quick View screen:desktop-quick-view.webp \"Quick View\" 1. Open Settings, Quick Entry / View and tick Enable Quick View . 2. Under List , choose what it shows: All projects , Today, Upcoming, Anytime, one project, or one of your custom lists guide:custom-lists . You can also tick Include tasks due today from all projects . 3. Press the hotkey: Alt + Shift + B by default, Cmd + Shift + B on a Mac. Inside the window: Key What it does --- --- Up and Down Move through the tasks Enter Complete the selected task. Press it again to undo. Tab Show or hide the task's description Shift + Enter Edit the title and description ! Set the due date to today, or remove it if there is one Esc Close the window Quick View follows your theme, picks up changes you make in the main window, and applies the conditions of a custom list. Global shortcuts and Wayland The Quick Entry and Quick View shortcuts work in any app. If another program already uses a key combination, Settings says it could not register the shortcut. Record a different one. On Linux with Wayland, apps cannot grab keys on their own. Vicu asks the desktop through the GlobalShortcuts portal. If a consent dialog appears, accept it and try again. If it still fails, Settings shows a command for each window. Add it as a custom shortcut in your desktop's keyboard settings GNOME: Settings, Keyboard, Custom Shortcuts ; KDE: System Settings, Shortcuts, Custom Shortcuts and keep Vicu running in the tray. The shortcut then wakes the running copy. On Linux with Wayland, apps cannot grab keys on their own. Vicu asks the desktop through the GlobalShortcuts portal. If a consent dialog appears, accept it and try again. If it still fails, Settings shows a command for each window. Add it as a custom shortcut in your desktop's keyboard settings GNOME: Settings, Keyboard, Custom Shortcuts ; KDE: System Settings, Shortcuts, Custom Shortcuts and keep Vicu running in the tray. The shortcut then wakes the running copy. The tray, startup and the window - Tray icon. It exists while Quick Entry or Quick View is on. Click it to show Vicu, or right-click for Show Vicu , Quick Entry , Quick View and Quit . On macOS the menu opens with a single click. - Closing the window. With a tray icon or on a Mac , the close button hides Vicu and keeps it running so the shortcuts stay ready. Choose Quit from the tray, or File, Quit on Windows and Linux, to end it. - Launch on startup. Settings, General, Preferences, Launch on startup starts Vicu when you sign in. It is advised if you use Quick Entry or Quick View. - Start hidden. With launch on startup ticked, Start hidden opens Vicu without a window. It needs the tray icon turned on through Quick Entry or Quick View or a Mac, so there is a way back to the window. - Linux. The AppImage writes an autostart entry. The option is hidden when Vicu cannot tell which file to start, for example in a development build. On GNOME the tray icon needs the AppIndicator extension. - Updates. Vicu checks for a new version every 12 hours and tells you. How the window looks on each system System Window --- --- Windows 11 Native minimize, maximize and close buttons with Snap Layouts, and a translucent Mica sidebar version 22H2 and later Windows 10 The same native buttons and an opaque sidebar macOS The usual traffic-light buttons and a translucent sidebar Linux Vicu draws its own window buttons The command palette ! The command palette screen:desktop-palette.webp \"The command palette\" Press Ctrl + Shift + P Cmd + Shift + P on a Mac and type. One field finds tasks, projects, labels and lists, and runs actions. Use Up and Down to choose, Enter to run, Esc to close. Actions include New task , Say a task , Refresh , Toggle theme and Settings . When the matching feature is on, you also find New event , Dictate tasks , Brain dump , Ask Vicu , Make tasks from text , and the guided sessions Sort the Inbox , Sort out overdue , Waiting a long time , Start review and Weekly review . A smart list you hid from the sidebar still opens from the palette. Quick find The Quick find box sits at the top of the sidebar. Press Ctrl + F Cmd + F to jump to it. Tasks that match show as you type. The last row, \"Search all tasks for ...\", opens the full Search page, which also finds notes and finished tasks and has filters for date, status, labels and priority. Refresh F5 or Ctrl + R Cmd + R reads the page you are on again from your server. It also refreshes projects, labels, sharing, custom lists and synced settings, and it sends waiting changes first. View, Reload window Ctrl + Shift + R reloads the whole window instead. Right-click text menu Right-click in any text field for spelling suggestions, Cut , Copy , Paste and Select all . On selected text that you cannot edit, you get Copy . A task row has its own right-click menu. Browser and Obsidian links Both work from Quick Entry. In Settings, Quick Entry / View , set Page Linking browser or Note Linking Obsidian to Off , Ask or Always auto-link . With Ask , press Ctrl + L in Quick Entry to attach the link. - Browser. Vicu reads the address of the active browser tab. A bundled extension for Chrome, Edge, Brave, Vivaldi, Opera or Firefox makes it instant. Without it, Vicu reads the address bar, which takes a moment. Under Browser Integration , paste a Chrome Extension ID if you installed it, and use Re-register Bridge if the link stops working. On a Mac the first use asks you to let Vicu control the browser. On Linux only the extension works, because the desktop does not let apps look at other windows. - Obsidian. Vicu links the note you have open. It needs two Obsidian plugins, Local REST API and Advanced URI, and you enter the API Key , Vault Name and Port under Obsidian Integration . Use Test Connection to check. Obsidian links are not available on Linux, and the settings are hidden there. Both sections are greyed out until Quick Entry is on. Links into Vicu Other apps and scripts can open Vicu with a vicu:// link. See Vicu Pro guide:pro for the list of links. Related - Quick add guide:quick-add - Keyboard shortcuts guide:shortcuts - Settings guide:settings - Sync and offline guide:sync - Add tasks guide:capture"},{"s":"phone","t":"Vicu on your phone","d":"What the Android, iPhone and iPad apps add: widgets, sharing to Vicu, swipes, notifications, location reminders, the drawer, Pure black and the connection banner.","h":[["in-short","In short"],["widgets","Widgets"],["add-a-task-from-anywhere","Add a task from anywhere"],["share-to-vicu","Share to Vicu"],["swipes-and-gestures","Swipes and gestures"],["notifications","Notifications"],["location-reminders-and-places","Location reminders and places"],["the-drawer-and-the-bottom-bar","The drawer and the bottom bar"],["look","Look"],["the-connection-banner","The connection banner"],["android-and-ios-differences","Android and iOS differences"],["related","Related"]],"x":"In short - One app for Android, iPhone and iPad. It reads your tasks by the same rules as the desktop app, so a due date, a custom list or a task order means the same thing everywhere. - Add tasks from outside the app with widgets, the share sheet, a Quick Settings tile , Siri and the Control Center , and the shortcuts you get when you hold the app icon. - Swipe a task right to complete it and left to schedule it. You can change both in Settings, Gestures . - Reminders come as notifications with Mark Complete and Snooze 15 min . Location reminders fire when you arrive at or leave a place. - The drawer and the bottom bar are yours to arrange. Settings, General holds most choices. - Offline is normal. A banner says when the phone cannot reach the server, and waiting changes are sent when it can. Widgets Add a widget the way you add any widget on your phone. Widget What it does Where --- --- --- Tasks Shows any list, smart, project or custom, and lets you tick tasks off Routines Shows today's routines with their times. Tap the circle to log one. Quick add A small widget with a microphone and a + button. The microphone adds a task by voice without opening the app. - On Android the widgets follow the Vicu colors or your wallpaper colors, and they refresh right after a tap. When you add the Tasks widget, its setup screen also offers Transparent background . - On both Settings, General, Widget has Smart add button the + adds to the widget's own project or list and Context navigation tapping the title opens that list . - On iPhone and iPad you choose the list in Edit Widget , and the Tasks widget comes in small, medium and large. The + button is in the medium and large sizes. A small widget has one tap target and opens the list. A tap in a widget is stored and sent the next time the app runs, so it can take a moment to reach the server. Add a task from anywhere - Hold the app icon and choose Add task or Dictate . - Quick Settings tile. Open Settings, Notifications, Quick Add tile and tap Add tile . The tile then opens the task entry from the notification shade. - Add task control. On iOS 18, add the control to Control Center, the Lock Screen or the Action Button. Settings, Notifications shows how. - Siri. Say \"Hey Siri, add a task in Vicu\". Siri asks \"What's the task?\" and adds it without opening the app, also on a locked phone. The words are read like quick add. In the Shortcuts app you can pair \"Dictate Text\" with Vicu's \"Add a task\" for a one-step Action Button. - TalkBack. The + button has a Say a task action. - Hold + and speak. Hold the + button, say the task, and let go to add it. Slide down to cancel. Share to Vicu Share a link, a photo, a video or a file from any app and choose Vicu. - Android opens the full task entry, with the title and files filled in. - iPhone and iPad show a small native sheet with the title, notes and attachments. Tap Add , and the task goes into the Inbox the next time Vicu runs or gets a connection. Quick add's parsing does not apply to a shared title on iOS. - A shared screenshot starts with an empty title. - If you shared text and the assistant guide:assistant is set up, Make tasks turns it into task cards. Swipes and gestures By default, swiping right completes a task and swiping left opens Choose when . To change them, open Settings, Gestures , tap Swipe Right or Swipe Left , and pick one of: Complete , Choose when , Due today , Due tomorrow , Mark urgent , Move to project , Delete , or Nothing . A swipe needs a drag of half the row's width. Delete always asks first. Other gestures: Gesture What it does --- --- Tap Opens the task Long press Selects several tasks Long press and drag Reorders tasks where the order is manual, and projects, lists and labels in the drawer Tap the checkbox Completes the task. Tap it again within a few seconds to undo. Pull down at the top Refreshes With TalkBack, every row offers Move up and Move down instead of dragging. Notifications Open Settings, Notifications . The app asks for the notification permission after setup, and again when you turn on something that needs it. - Task reminders. Each has Mark Complete and Snooze 15 min . A reminder never reopens a task that is already done. - Daily Summary. A morning notification, and optionally an Afternoon Summary , that count overdue, due today and with Include Tomorrow due tomorrow tasks. - Default Reminder. Added to every new task that has a due date and no reminder of its own. You choose the offset and what it is relative to due, start or end date . - Send Test Notification checks that it all works. Android checks the task on the server just before a reminder rings, and syncs about every 30 minutes in the background. iPhone and iPad plan up to 60 of the soonest reminders ahead, because iOS keeps only a limited number waiting, and sync when iOS allows it. A reminder for a task you completed on another device can still show until the iPhone has synced. See Android and iOS differences guide:phone android-and-ios-differences . Android checks the task on the server just before a reminder rings, and syncs about every 30 minutes in the background. iPhone and iPad plan up to 60 of the soonest reminders ahead, because iOS keeps only a limited number waiting, and sync when iOS allows it. A reminder for a task you completed on another device can still show until the iPhone has synced. See Android and iOS differences guide:phone android-and-ios-differences . Location reminders and places A task can remind you when you arrive at or leave a place. Open the task, tap the reminder, and choose At a location . 1. Pick a saved place, search for an address, or use where you are now. 2. Choose Arrive or Leave and a radius of 100, 200 the default or 500 meters. 3. The reminder fires once, also with the app closed, and never for a task that is done. The notification says \"Arriving at Home\" or \"Leaving Work\" and has Mark Complete and Snooze 15 min . - Save places in Settings, Notifications, Places , or choose Save as place when you set a reminder. In quick add or when you speak, when I get home and when I leave work set one for a saved place. - Privacy. Only a place's name and your reminders sync. Where a place is stays on the phone that saved it. Another phone lists it as \"Not set on this phone\" until you tap Set location . The desktop shows your places and can set a reminder at one, but only phones fire them. - Permission. Vicu explains first, then asks for location access set to Always . A reminder set while you are already at the place waits for the next arrival. - Limits. iOS can watch 20 places at once and Android 100. Address search sends only what you typed Android uses Photon and OpenStreetMap, iOS uses Apple Maps . The drawer and the bottom bar - Drawer. It lists your smart lists, projects, categories and labels. Hold and drag projects and labels to reorder them. Settings, General, Drawer hides the views you do not use. - Bottom bar. The Inbox always stays first. Settings, General, Bottom Bar sets the other three slots to any smart list, project or custom list, with an icon and a color. Smart list colours draws the Inbox, Today, Upcoming and Anytime icons in their own colors. Plan can take a slot when it is on. - Left-handed add button. Settings, General, Preferences can move the + button to the bottom-left corner. Look - Appearance in Settings, General : Theme System, Light or Dark and Task rows Compact or Comfortable . - Pure black. A black background in the dark theme for OLED screens. It shows when the theme is not Light. - Use device colours. Follows your wallpaper's palette Android 12 and later instead of the Vicu colors. The connection banner A neutral banner under the status bar says Offline or Can't reach the server , with the number of waiting changes. Airplane mode counts as offline. Nothing is lost: see Sync and offline guide:sync . Android and iOS differences Topic Android iPhone and iPad --- --- --- Quick add entry points Quick Settings tile, Quick add widget Control Center, Lock Screen and Action Button control, Siri Sharing Opens the full task entry A native sheet that adds to the Inbox Reminders Checked on the server when they ring Up to 60 planned ahead Background sync About every 30 minutes When iOS allows it. Settings, General, Background activity shows the last runs. Pasting an image into notes Supported Use Add image instead Device colors Supported Not available Single sign-on In-app window or your browser An in-app web view. Some providers, Google among them, refuse it: choose Sign in through your browser , a password or an API token. Voice Android speech recognizer Apple speech recognizer, and Siri Related - Sync and offline guide:sync - Settings guide:settings - Add tasks guide:capture - Plan your day guide:plan - Quick add guide:quick-add"},{"s":"sync","t":"Sync and offline","d":"How Vicu keeps your devices in step through your Vikunja server, what syncs and what stays on one device, and what happens when you are offline.","h":[["in-short","In short"],["how-devices-stay-in-step","How devices stay in step"],["what-syncs-and-what-stays-on-one-device","What syncs and what stays on one device"],["the-hidden-tasks","The hidden tasks"],["the-offline-queue","The offline queue"],["the-sync-status-button","The sync status button"],["on-the-phone","On the phone"],["signing-out-and-signing-in-as-someone-else","Signing out and signing in as someone else"],["standalone-mode","Standalone mode"],["sessions-in-vikunja","Sessions in Vikunja"],["details","Details"],["related","Related"]],"x":"In short - The server holds your data. Vicu has no server of its own. Each device keeps a copy to work from and stays in step with your Vikunja server. - Tasks, lists and many settings follow you. Custom lists, your Inbox choice, categories, templates, task order, Events and more sync. Look and behavior settings stay on each device. - Offline works. You can add, edit and complete tasks without a connection. The changes wait in a queue and go out in order when you are back. - Only what you changed is sent. An edit on your phone never undoes one you made on your computer. - You can see what is waiting. On the desktop, a status button appears in the sidebar. On the phone, a banner and Settings, General show it. How devices stay in step Each Vicu app asks the server for fresh data and sends your changes to it. - Vicu refreshes when you return to the window at most every 30 seconds , every 5 minutes while you look at it, after your computer wakes up, and at midnight so Today rolls over. Press F5 to refresh the page on request. - The app syncs when you open it, when you pull down on a list, and in the background: about every 30 minutes on Android, and when iOS allows it on iPhone and iPad. Reminders and widgets update after each sync. What syncs and what stays on one device Follows you to every device and to the Vikunja web app stored in your server's own fields : tasks, projects, labels, due and start dates, reminders, attachments, comments and assignees. Follows you to every device through Vicu stored on your server in hidden tasks, see below : - Custom lists - Routines and their history - Your Inbox project - Task order, per project and global, and the order of subtasks - Project categories and project icons - Task templates - Your own Events, the Plan default length and your work hours - Saved places names only and location reminders - The date of your last weekly review - Which shared routines you hid or muted - Vicu Pro data, while you use it Stays on each device: - Theme, clock, task row density and the other look settings - Quick-add syntax, notification choices, hotkeys, Quick Entry and Quick View - Whether Plan, Pro and the assistant are turned on, and the assistant's provider and key - Connected calendars and which ones you show - On the phone: the bottom bar, widgets and swipes - Where a saved place is. Only its name travels. Settings guide:settings marks every setting as synced or per device. The hidden tasks Vikunja has no place for an app's own data, so Vicu keeps custom lists, routines, synced settings and Pro records in tasks that it marks done and hides everywhere in Vicu. You may see them in other Vikunja apps. Names include \"Vicu preferences sync metadata — do not delete \". Leave them in place. Deleting one deletes that data. Each person's Vicu reads only the hidden tasks that person created, so sharing an Inbox with someone does not mix your settings. The offline queue Without a connection, Vicu keeps working from the copy it holds. The app opens with your last lists, and these changes wait in a queue: creating, editing, completing and deleting tasks, labels, pasted images and files, comments, assignees, and project edits. - Changes go out in the order you made them, one at a time. - A task created offline gets a temporary id and is sent first, with its labels and files after it. - If a send fails because of the network or the server, Vicu retries after 5 seconds, then 15 seconds, then every minute. - Waiting changes are sent within seconds of getting a connection back. - A create that timed out may already have reached the server. Vicu says so and asks you to check before trying again. Some things need a connection: sharing a project or a routine, uploading a file to a comment, and creating new projects from a brain dump. The sync status button When something is waiting or has failed, a button appears at the bottom of the sidebar. It shows how many changes are waiting, how many failed, and whether you need to sign in again. Click it to open Sync status . - Waiting to sync lists changes not sent yet, with Sync now . - Failed lists changes the server refused, with the reason, and Retry all and Discard all or the same for one change . The server refuses a change when, for example, the task was deleted elsewhere, a file is too large, you may no longer edit that project, or your API token is not allowed to do it. - Lists and settings shows custom-list and settings changes that have not reached the server, with Sync now and Discard . Discard sets them back to what the server holds. - If your session expired, it asks you to sign in again. Waiting changes are kept and sent once you are in. When everything is synced the panel says \"Everything is synced\" and the button hides. On the phone Settings, General, Data & Sync shows whether you are Connected or Offline and how many changes are pending or failed. Sync Now sends them, Retry All tries the failed ones again, and Clear Failed drops them. Clear Cache & Re-sync deletes the local data and fetches everything from the server, with an option to discard unsynced changes too. A failed change is dropped after 14 days. The connection banner guide:phone the-connection-banner tells you when the phone is offline. Signing out and signing in as someone else - Signing out first tries to send what is waiting. If something has not reached the server, Vicu asks before it signs you out. Unsynced custom-list and settings edits would be lost. Queued task changes stay for the next sign-in to the same account. - A different user or server. Signing in as someone else clears the previous account's custom lists, Inbox choice and synced data, so two people on one computer never see each other's lists. Device settings such as the theme stay. Changes made for one account are never sent to another. - Sign-in expired. If your session runs out, Vicu keeps your changes and asks you to sign in again. Sign in the same way you did before. If you used the browser sign-in, it starts that way again. Standalone mode Earlier versions of the desktop app could keep tasks on the computer only, without a server. New setups always connect to a server, but if you still use that mode, it keeps working: it has no Plan screen, no time blocks, no Pro, and no synced settings. When you later connect to a server, Vicu offers to upload the local tasks to your Inbox. Sessions in Vikunja Each device that signs in appears under Settings, Sessions in Vikunja, named like \"Vicu/3.0.0 Windows; HOSTNAME \" or \"Vicu/3.0.0 Android; Pixel 8 \", so you can tell them apart and end one. Password and single sign-on logins also create a backup API token titled \"Vicu — host id \", valid for 365 days. Signing out revokes it. Details - No overwrites. Each edit sends only the fields you changed. An edit made on another device in the meantime stays. - Writes go one at a time , because Vikunja's default database answers parallel writes with \"database is locked\". - Many tasks at once. If the network fails partway through a bulk change, the remaining tasks go straight to the queue instead of each waiting for a timeout. - Servers with a broken search. Some Vikunja servers answer searches with an error. Custom lists, settings and routines still load. Related - Settings guide:settings - Vicu on your phone guide:phone - Vicu on the desktop guide:desktop - Troubleshooting and FAQ guide:faq"},{"s":"settings","t":"Settings","d":"Every setting in the Vicu desktop and phone apps, what it does, and whether it follows you to your other devices or stays on one.","h":[["in-short","In short"],["desktop","Desktop"],["general","General"],["projects","Projects"],["quick-entry-view","Quick Entry / View"],["notifications","Notifications"],["assistant","Assistant"],["pro","Pro"],["keyboard-shortcuts","Keyboard Shortcuts"],["phone","Phone"],["general-2","General"],["projects-2","Projects"],["notifications-2","Notifications"],["gestures","Gestures"],["assistant-2","Assistant"],["accessibility","Accessibility"],["settings-that-always-sync","Settings that always sync"],["related","Related"]],"x":"In short - Open Settings from the sidebar desktop or the drawer phone . The desktop has tabs: General , Projects , Quick Entry / View , Notifications , Assistant , Pro and Keyboard Shortcuts . The phone has General , Projects , Notifications , Gestures and Assistant . - Most settings are per device. Look, behavior, notification and shortcut choices stay on the device where you set them. Setting the theme on your phone does not change your computer. - Some settings are synced. They live on your server and appear on every device you sign in on: your Inbox, task order, custom lists, categories, templates, planner defaults, and a few more. Tables below mark them Synced . - Settings save as you change them. There is no Save button. - Only a few shortcuts can be changed. See Keyboard shortcuts guide:shortcuts . How to read the tables: Synced follows you to every device. This device stays where you set it. Desktop General Connection Setting What it does Follows you --- --- --- Vikunja URL The server Vicu talks to. Shown, not editable, when you signed in with a password or single sign-on. This device Sign Out Signs out. First sends what is waiting and asks if anything has not reached the server. This device API Token and Test Connection Only when you signed in with a token. Paste a new one to replace it. Disconnect removes it. This device Appearance Setting What it does Follows you --- --- --- Theme Light, dark or system This device Clock System, 12-hour or 24-hour This device Task rows Compact one title line or Comfortable up to three title lines and a line of notes This device Preferences Setting What it does Follows you --- --- --- Launch on startup Starts Vicu when you sign in to your computer This device Start hidden Starts without a window. Needs the tray icon. This device Confirm before deleting Asks before deleting tasks, projects, labels and lists This device Subtasks in task lists Inside task keeps them in the task, Expandable shows them under the parent This device Show today & overdue count on app icon A badge with the number of tasks due today and overdue This device What does urgent mean? Schedule for Today or Mark Important : what the top action in a task's right-click menu does This device Sidebar Tick the smart lists you want above your projects. The Inbox always stays. A hidden list still opens from the command palette guide:desktop the-command-palette . This choice is per computer. Task Completion Sound A sound when you complete a task. Pick a file MP3, WAV, OGG, M4A, AAC or FLAC, up to 10 MB , use Play to hear it, or reset to the default. This device. Routines Setting What it does Follows you --- --- --- Enable routines Off hides Routines, its Today section and its reminders. Nothing is deleted. This device Show routines in Today Lists the routines still open today This device Routines shown on Today How many due routines show: 1, 2 the default , 3, 5 or all. The rest wait behind a line such as \"2 later today\". This device Hidden shared routines Routines someone shared that you hid, with a way to show them again Synced See Routines guide:routines . Task Parser Setting What it does Follows you --- --- --- Parse task metadata from text Reads labels, projects, priority, dates and repeats from what you type. Off makes the title plain text. This device Syntax Mode Todoist style project , @label , p1 or Vikunja style +project , label , !1 This device ! in task title sets due date to today A lone ! before or after a title means due today This device See Quick add guide:quick-add . Projects Setting What it does Follows you --- --- --- Inbox project Where new tasks land. Setup suggests the one another device already chose. Synced Project list Your projects and categories, with New project and New category , drag to reorder, and each project's menu Synced Sort project tasks by The global order: Due date then manual, Due date then priority, Manual, Priority, Title, Newest first, Oldest first. A project can have its own. Synced Show project progress in the sidebar A ring per project This device Show task count in the sidebar Open tasks per project. Off until you turn it on. This device Enable project review tracking Turns the review list on This device Default review cadence days How often projects are due for review. 14 to start. This device Exclude Inbox from review list Leaves the Inbox out This device Excluded from review Projects you left out, with Include again Synced stored with the project Import saved filters Turns your Vikunja saved filters into custom lists Synced the lists See Projects and labels guide:projects , Custom lists guide:custom-lists and Review and guided organizing guide:review . Quick Entry / View All of these are This device . See On the desktop guide:desktop . Setting What it does --- --- Enable Quick Entry, Enable Quick View Each is off until you turn it on Hotkey Quick Entry Default Alt + Shift + V , Cmd + Shift + Space on a Mac Default Project Where Quick Entry saves Secondary Projects, Project Cycle Modifier The projects you can switch to, and the key that does it Hotkey Quick View Default Alt + Shift + B , Cmd + Shift + B on a Mac List What Quick View shows Include tasks due today from all projects Adds today's tasks from every project Browser Integration Page Linking , Chrome Extension ID, Re-register Bridge Obsidian Integration Note Linking , API Key, Vault Name, Port, Test Connection . Not on Linux. Notifications All This device , except the list of places. Setting What it does --- --- Enable desktop notifications The master switch. On a Mac, the first notification asks for permission. Scheduled Reminders A Morning reminder and an Afternoon reminder with a time each. Include Overdue tasks , Tasks due today , Tasks due tomorrow . Play sound and Stay until dismissed . Task Reminders Play sound and Stay until dismissed for reminders you set on tasks Default reminder Added to every new task that has a due date and no reminder. Relative to the due, start or end date. Never added to existing tasks. Test Notification Sends a test Places The places you saved on your phone, read only. Synced. Assistant All This device : the assistant's provider, key and models do not sync. See The assistant guide:assistant . Section What it holds --- --- Thinking Provider OpenAI, Anthropic, Google Gemini, OpenRouter, Ollama, Open WebUI or another compatible server , address, key, Model , and Use a different model for Ask Features Switches for Smart dictation , Brain dump , Ask and Talk , each with Try it Hearing Speech models to download so voice is recognized on your computer, and the Language Privacy Delete conversations Pro Turn Pro on for this device, choose its parts, and set Assistant help in Pro . Those choices are per device. The work area, the cap on active initiatives and the escalation rule are synced. See Vicu Pro guide:pro . Plan Plan sits on the Pro tab, but works whether Pro is on or off. Shown unless you use standalone mode. See Plan your day guide:plan and Events and calendars guide:calendars . Setting What it does Follows you --- --- --- Show Plan in the sidebar Turns the Plan screen on. Off by default. A time you set on a task shows on every device either way. This device Default length What a task takes when you drop it on a time Synced Work hours Start and end of your day, used for free time, Fit and Plan my day Synced Ask me to plan tomorrow An evening notification at the time you choose This device Show routines on the timeline Routine pills on Plan This device Calendars Connected calendars, their colors, Busy only, and Import a calendar file This device Keyboard Shortcuts Shows the shortcut list, and the Voice input shortcut This device , off until you record one . See Keyboard shortcuts guide:shortcuts . Phone Settings are in the drawer. Everything on the phone is per device unless marked. General Section What it holds Follows you --- --- --- Account Username, email, auth method, server, Sign Out This device Appearance Theme System, Light, Dark , Pure black , Use device colours only , Task rows Compact or Comfortable This device Bottom Bar The last three slots, Smart list colours , Reset to Defaults This device Plan Show Plan in the drawer , and the Plan options default length and work hours sync, the evening nudge does not Switch: this device. Length and hours: Synced Pro Turn on Pro on this device and its parts This device Drawer Which views the drawer lists This device Widget Smart add button , Context navigation This device Routines Enable routines , Show routines in Today , Routines shown on Today , Hidden shared routines This device. Hidden shared routines: Synced Logbook Hide old completed tasks and Keep completed tasks for a number of days. Nothing is deleted. This device Input Parsing Natural Language Parsing , Syntax Mode Todoist or Vikunja , and \"Add ! to set the due date to today\" This device Preferences Confirm before deleting , Subtasks in task lists , Keep add-task open , Left-handed add button , Play sound when completing a task This device Data & Sync Status, Sync Now , Retry All , Clear Failed , Clear Cache & Re-sync , Background activity n/a Projects Section What it holds Follows you --- --- --- Inbox Project Choose inbox project , and Move dated tasks out of Inbox Inbox: Synced. The move option: This device Display Task order and Sort tasks per project , Subprojects in project views , Show project progress , Show task count Order: Synced. The rest: This device Review Enable project review tracking , Default review cadence , Exclude Inbox from review , and the list of excluded projects This device Projects, Labels Your projects tap to edit, hold to drag, ⋮ for more and labels Synced Custom Lists Your custom lists, and the import of saved filters Synced Notifications See Vicu on your phone guide:phone notifications . This device : Task Reminders , Sound , Daily Summary and its times and counts, Default Reminder , Send Test Notification , the Quick Add tile or Add task control hint . Places sync by name, and positions stay on the phone. Gestures Swipe Right and Swipe Left , each set to one action. This device. Assistant Provider, key, models, features and speech models. This device. Parakeet is the suggested speech model on phones. Downloads wait for Wi-Fi unless you allow mobile data. Accessibility Vicu follows your system's accessibility settings; there is nothing to turn on in Vicu. - Keyboard : everything works from the keyboard. Lists move with the arrow keys, menus and pickers jump to an item as you type its name, and one focus ring shows where you are. See Keyboard shortcuts guide:shortcuts . - Screen readers : menus, dialogs and lists are real controls with names, and changes such as \"Completed\" are announced. On the phone, every task row has screen reader actions, such as Complete, Due today and Due tomorrow. - Less motion : when your system asks for reduced motion, Vicu replaces movement with simple fades. On Windows that is Settings, Accessibility, Visual effects, Animation effects . - Contrast themes : with a Windows contrast theme, Vicu uses the system's colors and keeps every meaning visible by shape or text, not color alone. Priority, for example, is shown by bars and a mark, not only by color. - Text size : Vicu follows Dynamic Type. Settings that always sync For quick reference, these follow your account to every device: - The Inbox project - Task order, global and per project, and subtask order - Custom lists and imported saved filters - Project categories and icons - Task templates - Plan default length and work hours - Your own Events - Saved place names and location reminders - Hidden shared routines and routine reminder choices - The date of your last weekly review - Pro's work area, cap and escalation rule, and Pro data Everything else stays on the device. Sync and offline guide:sync explains how this works. Related - Sync and offline guide:sync - Keyboard shortcuts guide:shortcuts - Quick add guide:quick-add - On the desktop guide:desktop - On your phone guide:phone"},{"s":"pro","t":"Vicu Pro","d":"Vicu Pro is an optional switch in Vicu for more complex work: what you can do now, promises people made you, initiatives you steer, and faster tools.","h":[["in-short","In short"],["watch-the-tour","Watch the tour"],["why-pro-exists","Why Pro exists"],["who-it-is-for","Who it is for"],["the-four-parts","The four parts"],["plan-and-calendars","Plan and calendars"],["turn-pro-on","Turn Pro on"],["try-it-safely","Try it safely"],["pro-on-the-phone","Pro on the phone"],["where-pro-keeps-its-data","Where Pro keeps its data"],["links-into-vicu","Links into Vicu"],["related","Related"]],"x":"In short - Pro is optional and free. It is built into Vicu and off until you turn it on in Settings. - It only adds. With Pro off, Vicu looks and works exactly as it always did. Turning Pro off changes nothing and deletes nothing; turn it on again and everything is where you left it. - It comes in four parts that you turn on one by one: Know what you can do now, Promises and people, Portfolio, and Power tools. - Your data stays on your server , next to your tasks, like the rest of Vicu. - The phone has the first two parts ; the desktop has all four. The phone keeps the other parts' data safe and follows it. - This is an alpha. Things will change. Keep your Vikunja server backed up while you try it. Watch the tour ! Getting started with Vicu Pro: a tour of the four parts video:pro-getting-started.mp4 \"Getting started with Vicu Pro, about five minutes. No sound: the captions are in the picture.\" The tour takes one part at a time: turning Pro on, what you can do now, promises and people, the portfolio, and the power tools. You do not need all of it on day one. Why Pro exists Vicu is built to be calm: a few lists, quick capture, a clear Today. That suits most days. But some work is more tangled. You lead a team or a project with others. People promise you things in meetings and you need to know, a week later, whether they happened. Half your tasks cannot start until something else is done. You steer several initiatives and keep being asked to take on one more. Putting tools for all of that into everyone's Vicu would make it heavier for the people who never need them. So they live behind one switch. You decide when your work needs them, and how much of it. Who it is for Pro is for you if several of these sound familiar: - You lead people, a team or a cross-team project, and depend on what others deliver. - You run 1:1s or recurring meetings and want each one to start from what was agreed last time. - You have more ideas and requests than capacity, and want to say no or \"not now\" with reasons. - Your lists are long, and you want to see only what you can actually do right now. - You like keyboard shortcuts, tables and doing ten things at once. If none of this is you, you do not need Pro, and Vicu is not less without it. The four parts Each part works on its own. Start with one and add more when you feel the need. Part What it adds Complexity Where --- --- --- --- Know what you can do now guide:pro/available Available, Remaining and All on your lists; start dates; waiting on other tasks; step-by-step projects; on hold; flags; estimates; Focus Low: a few new fields and a filter Promises and people guide:pro/promises People, promises both ways, verdicts, Waiting on, the person page, 1:1 agendas, recaps, meetings, import from Copilot Medium: a habit of giving verdicts meetings and import on the desktop Portfolio guide:pro/portfolio Initiatives with a cap, rank and parking; readiness; escalations; the Sponsor desk; request triage High: a way of running your work Power tools guide:pro/power Pro conditions and grouping in custom lists, a table view, the inspector, estimates and a focus block in Plan my day, the weekly execution review, a quarterly impact summary Medium: power tools Assistant help in Pro is a separate switch, off by default. With it on, the assistant guide:assistant can see Pro's data: Ask can look up promises, people and the portfolio, an import can convert plain notes, and a meeting can draft promises from what was said. With it off, the assistant sees your tasks as before and none of Pro's records. Plan and calendars The Plan screen guide:plan and Events and calendars guide:calendars are listed here too, and their settings are in Settings, Pro, Plan on the desktop on the phone, right after Pro in Settings, General . They do not need Pro: Plan works the same with Pro on or off. With Power tools on, Plan my day also uses your estimates and proposes a focus block. Turn Pro on Pro is per device: turn it on on each device where you want it. What Pro stores is shared through your server, so a promise you note on the desktop is on your phone too. Under Start dates , Vicu tells you how many of your open tasks already have a start date set in the Vikunja web app, for example . With Pro on, those tasks wait until that day; Show them lists them. Pro does not work in standalone mode: it needs a Vikunja server. A good way in: turn on only Know what you can do now for a week. If seeing just what you can do helps, add Promises and people next. A good way in: turn on only Know what you can do now for a week. If seeing just what you can do helps, add Promises and people next. Try it safely - Turning Pro off writes nothing. Lists show everything again, the Pro screens leave the sidebar, and your Pro data waits on the server. - Other devices keep your Pro data safe , also where Pro is off, also an older Vicu, and also the Vikunja web app: Pro uses Vikunja's own fields where they exist start dates, the favorite flag, labels, task relations and keeps the rest in one hidden task. - Back up your server. Pro is an alpha; a backup of your Vikunja database is the safety net for any alpha software. - Work data and your employer. Using Pro for work puts work information on your own server, and, with Assistant help in Pro on, in requests to the AI provider you chose. Check that your employer is fine with both. Pro on the phone The phone app has Know what you can do now and Promises and people : the view switch and reasons on every list, Pro's fields in the task editor, Flagged, Waiting on, People and the person page, promises due on Today with verdicts, and capture of promise words. Meetings, import, the Portfolio and the Power tools are on the desktop. The phone still honors their data: a parked initiative's tasks stay out of the way, and a custom list's Pro conditions work there too. Where Pro keeps its data Most of what Pro adds is stored in Vikunja's own fields, so other apps see it: - Start dates are the task's start date. - Flags are Vikunja's favorite star. - People are labels you marked as a person. - \"After\" is a Vikunja task relation blocked by . The rest promises and their verdicts, initiatives, logs, meetings, estimates and Pro's settings lives in one hidden task in your Inbox, titled \"Vicu Pro ledger sync metadata — do not delete \". Vicu hides it everywhere. Do not delete it in the Vikunja web app: that would delete those records. Older records move into hidden archive tasks so it stays small. Links into Vicu Other apps, mail rules and scripts can open Vicu with a vicu:// link: Link Opens --- --- vicu://task/123 Task 123 in the main window vicu://view/flagged A list: inbox , today , upcoming , anytime , logbook , plan , review , routines , flagged , waiting vicu://add?title=...&project=...&due=... Quick Entry filled in; it never adds a task on its own Links work in the installed app. The Pro lists flagged , waiting open only while Pro is on. Related - Know what you can do now guide:pro/available - Promises and people guide:pro/promises - Portfolio guide:pro/portfolio - Power tools guide:pro/power - Keyboard shortcuts guide:shortcuts"},{"s":"pro/available","t":"Know what you can do now","d":"The first part of Vicu Pro: see only the tasks you can act on, with start dates, tasks that wait on other tasks, step-by-step projects, on hold, flags and Focus.","h":[["in-short","In short"],["available-remaining-and-all","Available, Remaining and All"],["why-a-task-is-not-available","Why a task is not available"],["start-a-task-later","Start a task later"],["make-a-task-wait-on-another","Make a task wait on another"],["run-a-project-step-by-step","Run a project step by step"],["put-a-project-or-label-on-hold","Put a project or label on hold"],["flag-what-matters","Flag what matters"],["estimate-how-long-a-task-takes","Estimate how long a task takes"],["focus-on-one-area","Focus on one area"],["repeat-with-an-end","Repeat with an end"],["details","Details"],["related","Related"]],"x":"In short - Lists show Available by default: the open tasks you can do right now. Remaining shows every open task, All adds the done ones. - A task waits when it starts later , comes after another task, is later in a sequence , sits in a project or under a label on hold , or is someone else's promise . The row says which. - Flags mark the few tasks that matter most; the Flagged list gathers them. - Estimates say how long a task takes, and Focus narrows the whole app to one area of work. - Everything here is stored in Vikunja's own fields or synced with your account, so it shows on every device where Pro is on. This page is part of Vicu Pro guide:pro . Turn on Pro and the part Know what you can do now in Settings, Pro on the desktop or Settings, General, Pro on the phone. This page is part of Vicu Pro guide:pro . Turn on Pro and the part Know what you can do now in Settings, Pro on the desktop or Settings, General, Pro on the phone. Available, Remaining and All With this part on, projects, labels, Anytime, custom lists and Flagged get a switch at the top: Choice Shows --- --- Available Open tasks you can do now Remaining Every open task, including the ones that wait All Open and done tasks Available is where every list starts. The choice is remembered per list on this device, so a project can stay on Remaining while the rest show Available. When Available leaves tasks out, a line at the end of the list says so, for example \"3 tasks are not available yet.\", with Show remaining to see them. Today and Upcoming are not filtered this way: they are about dates, and a task you dated for today shows there. Why a task is not available A task that waits says why in its row: Row says Why it waits What makes it available --- --- --- Starts Thursday It has a start date later than today That day arriving After: Book the venue It waits on another task and \"and 2 more\" when it waits on several Those tasks being done Later in sequence Its project is sequential and this is not the first open task The tasks before it being done On hold Its project, or a project above it, is on hold or parked The project being made active Waiting One of its labels is on hold The label being made active Their promise It is a promise someone else made you Nothing to do: you give it a verdict when it is due A task can wait for more than one reason; the row shows the first. Start a task later A start date says \"not before this day\". Until then the task leaves Available, and on the day itself it shows on Upcoming and becomes available. - In the title, type starts monday , starts 12 dec or starts in 2 weeks . See Quick add guide:quick-add . - On an open task, use Start date and pick Tomorrow , Next week , In two weeks or a day. A time block counts as its own start: a task planned for Friday at 10:00 waits until Friday. While a task has a time block, its Start date chip is hidden. Start dates are Vikunja's own field. If you set start dates in the Vikunja web app before, those tasks start waiting when you turn this part on. Settings, Pro tells you how many there are, with Show them to list them. Start dates are Vikunja's own field. If you set start dates in the Vikunja web app before, those tasks start waiting when you turn this part on. Settings, Pro tells you how many there are, with Show them to list them. Make a task wait on another Some tasks cannot start until another one is done: send the invitations after the venue is booked. 1. Open the task that has to wait. 2. Choose After . 3. Find the task it waits on and pick it. Add more if it waits on several. The card says \"This task becomes available when these are done.\" As soon as the last one is done, the task is available. To stop waiting, remove the task from the list under After . \"After\" is a Vikunja task relation blocked by , so the Vikunja web app shows it too. When tasks wait on each other in a loop, Vicu ignores the loop and says so on the card; remove one of them. Run a project step by step A project can say how its tasks relate. On the desktop, open the project's menu and choose Type : - Parallel : every task can be done now. This is how projects work without Pro. - Sequential : only the first open task is available; the next one comes up when it is done. Use the project's order, so set Order tasks by hand Manual when the order matters same menu . - Single actions : a bag of separate tasks with no order, like Errands. Subtasks follow their parent: in a sequential project, the first open task's subtasks are available with it. Put a project or label on hold When something is paused, put it on hold instead of moving its tasks around. - A project : in its menu, Status , then On hold . Its tasks, and the tasks of its subprojects, wait with the reason On hold . Choose Active to bring them back. - A label : right-click it in the sidebar and choose Put on hold . Every task with that label waits with the reason Waiting . This suits labels like \"waiting for budget\". Make active ends it. Projects and labels on hold are synced with your account, so every device agrees. Flag what matters A flag marks the few tasks you do not want to lose sight of. - Type !flag in a title. - On the desktop, press Ctrl + Shift + F Cmd on a Mac on a row, or use Flag on the open task. - On the phone, use the flag in the task editor. Flagged in the sidebar lists every flagged task, grouped by project, with the same Available, Remaining and All switch. The flag is Vikunja's favorite star, so the web app shows it as a favorite. Estimate how long a task takes Type ~30m , ~1h or ~1h30 in the title, or use Estimate on the open task. Estimates are shown on the card and are used by Plan my day guide:plan and by custom lists that pick quick wins see Power tools guide:pro/power . Focus on one area Focus narrows the whole app to one area of your work, for example only your job's projects during the day. 1. Press Ctrl + Shift + O , or choose Focus in the command palette. 2. Tick the Categories or Projects to keep. 3. Confirm. The sidebar, the lists and search show only those projects until you leave Focus. Focus is per device \"Show only these on this device\" , and the Inbox always stays. While Focus is on, a line at the top of the sidebar names what is in focus: click it to change the choice, or click its x Clear focus to see everything again. Repeat with an end With Pro on, a repeating task can end. In the repeat settings, Ends offers Never , After times a number of times or On a day . Catch up: skip dates that have passed moves a repeating task you completed late to the next date still ahead, instead of every date you missed. Details - Where it is stored. Start dates, flags and \"After\" are Vikunja's own fields. A project's type and status and a label's status are synced with your account. Nothing is lost when you turn Pro off: lists show every task again and the values wait on your server. - The phone. The phone has this part, with the switch and reasons on its lists, and Start, After, Estimate and the flag in the task editor. Focus is desktop only; on the phone, the Work area setting limits Pro's promises to the categories you chose. - Custom lists. A custom list can pick tasks by availability, flag and estimate. See Power tools guide:pro/power . Related - What Pro is guide:pro - Promises and people guide:pro/promises - Smart lists guide:lists - Quick add syntax guide:quick-add"},{"s":"pro/promises","t":"Promises and people","d":"The second part of Vicu Pro: note what people promised you and what you promised them, give verdicts, see Waiting on, prepare 1:1s, run meetings and import notes.","h":[["in-short","In short"],["mark-a-label-as-a-person","Mark a label as a person"],["note-a-promise","Note a promise"],["give-a-verdict","Give a verdict"],["your-own-promises","Your own promises"],["see-what-others-owe-you","See what others owe you"],["prepare-a-11-on-the-person-page","Prepare a 1:1 on the person page"],["send-a-recap","Send a recap"],["run-a-meeting","Run a meeting"],["import-notes-from-copilot-or-another-assistant","Import notes from Copilot or another assistant"],["details","Details"],["related","Related"]],"x":"In short - People are labels you mark as a person. Nothing new to set up: the labels you already use for people work. - A promise is a task with a direction: someone promised it to you, or you promised it to them. Type Jan: send the deck friday or for Jan: review the budget . - When a promise is due , Vicu asks for a verdict : Delivered, Late, Not yet, New date or Dropped. Promises due are at the top of Today. - Waiting on lists what others owe you; the person page has everything with one person and the agenda for your next 1:1. - Recaps turn what was agreed into text for mail or chat. On the desktop you can also run meetings and import notes from Microsoft 365 Copilot or another assistant. This page is part of Vicu Pro guide:pro . Turn on Pro and the part Promises and people in Settings, Pro on the desktop or Settings, General, Pro on the phone. This page is part of Vicu Pro guide:pro . Turn on Pro and the part Promises and people in Settings, Pro on the desktop or Settings, General, Pro on the phone. Mark a label as a person 1. Right-click the label in the sidebar on the phone, open the label's menu . 2. Choose This is a person . The person now appears under People in the sidebar, with how many open promises go each way. To undo it, choose Not a person ; the label stays a normal label. A person can go by other names. When an import meets a name it does not know, it asks Who is this? , and the name you match is remembered for that person, so \"Jan\" and \"Jan de Vries\" end up in the same place. Note a promise The quickest way is the title, with the person's name and a colon at the very start: You type You get --- --- Jan: send the deck friday Jan's promise to you, due Friday for Jan: review the budget Your promise to Jan Priya de Boer: share the numbers tomorrow Priya's promise, due tomorrow The name must be a person Vicu knows a label marked as a person, one of their other names, or a first name only one person has . An unknown name stays in the title. The person's label is added to the task. You can also turn any task into a promise: open it and use Promise . Choose They promised me or I promised them , the person, and optionally: - In their words or What you said : the promise as it was said, so nobody argues later about what was meant. - We will see it is done when : what done looks like. A promise someone made you shows the person's mark in place of the checkbox, because it is not yours to tick. Your own promises keep their checkbox. A promise someone made you never counts as available, so it stays out of your Available lists see Know what you can do now guide:pro/available . If you set a Work area in Pro's settings, only promises in projects of those categories count. Use it to keep promises at home out of your work views. If you set a Work area in Pro's settings, only promises in projects of those categories count. Use it to keep promises at home out of your work views. Give a verdict When a promise someone made you is due, Vicu asks what happened. Promises due today or earlier are at the top of Today , and each has the verdict choices: Key Verdict Means --- --- --- 1 Delivered Done, on time 2 Late Done, but late 3 Not yet Not done. Pick when to Ask again on , and optionally What is in the way? 4 New date They asked for more time. Pick the new date 5 Dropped It will not happen 6 Ask me tomorrow You do not know yet On the desktop, focus the row and press the number, or click the choice. On the phone, tap the person's mark on the row. Every verdict can be undone from the message that follows. If the person completed the task themselves in a shared project, for example , Vicu asks once: Delivered by them, or I did it . A missed date counts. When a person misses the same promise twice, and you use the Portfolio guide:pro/portfolio , Vicu drafts an escalation for you to look at. Your own promises Your promises to others are tasks like any other: complete them when they are done. They are listed on the person's page under You owe them, so you see them before you meet. See what others owe you Waiting on in the sidebar lists the open promises others made you, grouped by person, the soonest first. Tasks with a label on hold for example \"waiting for budget\" are listed too. Prepare a 1:1 on the person page Click a person under People to open their page: - Next 1:1 : an agenda made from what is open between you: \"What's still unclear?\", their promises that are due or missed, what you owe them, and decisions they owe. - From them: what they promised you. - You owe them: what you promised. - To confirm : tasks named \"Confirm with\" them, made by an import or a triage when something they said was not a clear yes. Check it with them and complete the task. - A line about the last 90 days , in plain words, for example \"Kept 5 of 6 on time in the last 90 days, 1 late.\" It is never a score. On the desktop, Copy agenda puts the agenda on the clipboard for a meeting invite or chat. On the phone, share the agenda instead. Send a recap After a conversation, send what was agreed so everyone has the same list. 1. Open Recap on a person's page, from the command palette, or after an import or a meeting. 2. Choose the people and the days to include on the desktop, From a date; on the phone, the last 1, 7 or 30 days . 3. Copy it desktop or share it phone , and paste it into mail or chat. The promises in a copied recap are marked as sent, which the weekly review guide:pro/power uses to remind you of agreements nobody sent yet. Run a meeting For a team meeting or a round of 1:1s, a meeting goes through the people one at a time. 1. On People , choose New meeting . 2. Give it a name, add the People, in round order use Earlier and Later to change the order , and optionally a rhythm: weekly, every two weeks or monthly. 3. Save. The meeting is listed under Meetings on People. To run it, open the meeting and choose Start round . Each person gets a card with their open promises. Give verdicts as you go keys 1 to 6 on a promise and add new promises on the card. Your card: what you owe the room holds your own; type in You promise... and choose Add . Key Does --- --- Enter Next person S Skip this person A Mark this person absent There is no timer, and you can take the people in any order. Everything is written as it happens. End meeting writes the round who was discussed, skipped and absent and opens its recap. Import notes from Copilot or another assistant If your meetings are summarized by Microsoft 365 Copilot or another assistant , Vicu can read what it found: promises, your actions, decisions and open questions. After the import, Recap sends the people involved what was agreed. With Assistant help in Pro on, Import can also read plain notes that are not in the prompt's format Convert with the assistant , and a meeting card can draft promises from what was said. See the assistant guide:assistant . Details - What a promise is stored as. The task is a normal Vikunja task with the person's label. Its direction, words, verdicts and history are kept in Pro's hidden record on your server, which every device with Pro reads. - Turning this part off hides the person marks, verdicts and screens. The labels stay labels and the promise tasks stay tasks. - On the phone. People, Waiting on, the person page, promises due on Today, verdicts, recaps and capture of promise words are on the phone. When you note a promise there, it asks for In their words or What you said and when you will see it is done. Meetings and import are on the desktop. Related - What Pro is guide:pro - Know what you can do now guide:pro/available - Portfolio guide:pro/portfolio - Quick add syntax guide:quick-add - Work with others guide:collaboration"},{"s":"pro/portfolio","t":"Portfolio","d":"The third part of Vicu Pro, on the desktop: steer initiatives with a cap, a rank and parking, check readiness, draft escalations, triage requests and keep a Sponsor desk.","h":[["in-short","In short"],["make-a-project-an-initiative","Make a project an initiative"],["keep-the-active-ones-few","Keep the active ones few"],["park-an-initiative","Park an initiative"],["check-readiness","Check readiness"],["deal-with-missed-promises","Deal with missed promises"],["use-the-sponsor-desk","Use the Sponsor desk"],["triage-a-request","Triage a request"],["details","Details"],["related","Related"]],"x":"In short - An initiative is a project you are steering, with a sponsor and an owner. You make one from the project's menu. - The cap limits how many initiatives are active at once: four unless you agree on another number. To start a new one at the cap, you park one first, with a reason. - Readiness is a nine-point check on each initiative. Adding a task to one that is not ready asks first. - Escalations are drafted when a promise is missed twice; the Sponsor desk gathers them with the decisions you are owed and a one-pager. - Requests req: go through triage : Accept, Delegate, Decline or Ask the sponsor. This page is part of Vicu Pro guide:pro and works on the desktop. Turn on Pro and the part Portfolio in Settings, Pro . This page is part of Vicu Pro guide:pro and works on the desktop. Turn on Pro and the part Portfolio in Settings, Pro . Make a project an initiative 1. Open the project's menu the ... button in the sidebar, or right-click the project . 2. Choose Initiative , then Make this an initiative . The project now has an initiative panel at the top of its page, and it is listed on Portfolio in the sidebar. The same menu later offers Park or Make active , Close done and Not an initiative , which turns it back into a plain project. On the initiative panel: - Sponsor and Owner : the people who asked for it and who runs it people are labels you marked as a person; see Promises and people guide:pro/promises . Nobody yet until you choose. - Ready : how many of the nine readiness points are filled in. Open it to fill them in. - The log : note a Decision , a Value a result it delivered or an Open question as it happens. These feed the Sponsor desk, the weekly review and the quarterly impact. Keep the active ones few Portfolio in the sidebar lists your initiatives: - Active , in rank order. Use the arrows Move up , Move down to rank them: the top ones are where your time goes first. Each shows its owner and how ready it is for example Ready 6/9 . A bar at the top shows how many of the cap's places are taken. - Parked , with the reason each was parked. - Closed . The cap is four active initiatives. When you are at it, Portfolio says \"At the cap: park one before starting another\", and making another initiative active asks you to Choose one to park first. That is the point: something has to give, and Vicu makes it a decision instead of a drift. To change the cap, open Settings, Pro, Portfolio : set Active initiatives at most , and record who you Agreed with and when. A cap is only useful when the person who sponsors your work agreed to it. Park an initiative 1. On Portfolio, click the park button on the initiative, or choose Initiative, Park in the project's menu. 2. Give a reason, for example \"waiting for next quarter's budget\". 3. Choose Park . \"Its tasks wait until it is active again. The reason is kept with it.\" While parked, its tasks leave Today and Upcoming and show as On hold in your lists, on the desktop and on the phone. Choose Make active to bring it back. Check readiness An initiative that starts without the basics tends to stall. The readiness check has nine points: Point The question it answers --- --- Sponsor Who asked for this and can decide on it? Owner Who runs it day to day? Why now Why this, and why now? Done looks like How will we know it is finished? Smallest useful step What is the smallest result that is already useful? Not doing What is deliberately out of scope? Who decides what Which decisions are whose? Capacity agreed Did the sponsor agree to the time it takes? Use Mark agreed today Stop if When do we stop? Adding a task to an initiative that is not ready asks \"This initiative is not ready yet. Log the task anyway?\" Choose Log anyway to add it; Vicu counts how often that happens, which is worth raising with the sponsor. Deal with missed promises When someone misses the same promise twice, Vicu drafts an escalation : a short note for the sponsor that names the promise and how often it was missed. When the same person also has an open promise in another initiative, the draft asks which comes first, A or B. Otherwise it asks whether the sponsor can help unblock it, or whether what was agreed should change. You set after how many misses it drafts one in Settings, Pro, Portfolio , Draft an escalation after this many misses , and record when you agreed on the rule with Escalation rule agreed on . Escalations wait on the Sponsor desk . Nothing is sent by Vicu. Use the Sponsor desk Sponsor desk in the sidebar is your preparation for the next conversation with a sponsor: - Escalations : each draft with Put on the agenda , Copy , Resolved note what was decided and Dismiss . A decision you note is added to the log. - Decisions needed : questions you asked the sponsor, with how long they have been waiting. Choose Decided when you have an answer. - Capacity not agreed : initiatives whose sponsor has not agreed to the time yet. - Parked initiatives. The one-pager sums it up for the meeting: copy it into a document or mail, or print it. Triage a request A request is something someone asks you to take on. Instead of adding it to your plate straight away, weigh it against the ranked portfolio. 1. Type req: at the start of the title req: Ana wants a dashboard for sales , or right-click an existing task and choose Treat as request . 2. The triage window opens next to your ranked initiatives. Choose one: - Accept : Choose an initiative to put it in. - Delegate : say To whom . A \"Confirm with\" task reminds you to check they took it. - Decline : it is logged, so you can show what you said no to. - Ask the sponsor : it becomes a decision you are owed, on the Sponsor desk. 3. Choose Do it and copy the reply . A short reply for the person who asked is on your clipboard. Details - Turning this part off hides the panel, Portfolio and the Sponsor desk. Your initiatives, logs and escalations wait on your server. Parked initiatives stop hiding their tasks while the part is off. - On the phone. The Portfolio screens are desktop only. The phone follows parked initiatives, so their tasks stay out of Today and Upcoming there too. - Looking back. The weekly review and the quarterly impact summary read the portfolio. See Power tools guide:pro/power . Related - What Pro is guide:pro - Promises and people guide:pro/promises - Power tools guide:pro/power - Projects and labels guide:projects"},{"s":"pro/power","t":"Power tools","d":"The fourth part of Vicu Pro, on the desktop: Pro conditions and grouping in custom lists, the table, the inspector, estimates and a focus block in Plan my day, the weekly execution review and the quarterly impact.","h":[["in-short","In short"],["make-a-pro-list","Make a Pro list"],["start-from-a-ready-made-list","Start from a ready-made list"],["see-a-list-as-a-table","See a list as a table"],["change-many-tasks-at-once","Change many tasks at once"],["plan-my-day-with-estimates-and-a-focus-block","Plan my day with estimates and a focus block"],["the-weekly-review-for-execution","The weekly review, for execution"],["look-back-on-a-quarter","Look back on a quarter"],["related","Related"]],"x":"In short - Pro lists : custom lists can pick tasks by availability, flag, estimate, project status, promise and person, group them, and choose what each row shows. Four starter lists get you going. - The table : show a custom list as a table and sort by any column. - The inspector : set the start date, estimate and flag of many tasks at once. - Plan my day uses your estimates and proposes a focus block for your top initiatives. - The weekly review gets execution steps and a mirror line; Portfolio gets a quarterly impact summary. This page is part of Vicu Pro guide:pro and works on the desktop. Turn on Pro and the part Power tools in Settings, Pro . This page is part of Vicu Pro guide:pro and works on the desktop. Turn on Pro and the part Power tools in Settings, Pro . Make a Pro list Custom lists are the place for views of your own see Custom lists guide:custom-lists . With Power tools on, the list editor has a Pro section: Setting Choices --- --- Show Every task , Available now , Remaining open Flagged Any , Flagged , Not flagged Estimate at most minutes Only tasks estimated at this length or less Project status Any , Active , On hold Promise Any task , Promised to me , I promised , Either People Only tasks for these people Group by No groups , Project , Label , Person , Due date , Start date , Flagged Rows show Which details each row shows: Project , Labels , Due date , Start date , Estimate , Flag , Notes The editor counts the open tasks that match as you change the settings. Start from a ready-made list On a new list, Start from offers four lists you can use as they are or change: Starter What it shows --- --- Next actions Every available task, grouped by project Waiting on Promises others made you, grouped by person, by due date Quick wins Available tasks of 15 minutes or less, with project, due date and estimate Flagged by project Flagged tasks, grouped by project A list's Pro conditions are kept and followed on every device, Pro on or off, and on the phone too. A list made for \"Quick wins\" never suddenly shows everything somewhere else. The editor keeps showing the Pro section for such a list even where Power tools are off, so you can change it. A list's Pro conditions are kept and followed on every device, Pro on or off, and on the phone too. A list made for \"Quick wins\" never suddenly shows everything somewhere else. The editor keeps showing the Pro section for such a list even where Power tools are off, so you can change it. See a list as a table At the top of a custom list, switch between Rows and Table . - Click a column's header to sort by it; click again to reverse. - Shift +click a second column to sort by it within the first. - Rows select like list rows: click, Ctrl +click and Shift +click Cmd on a Mac , and open with a double click or Enter . Change many tasks at once The inspector sets Pro's fields of several tasks in one go. 1. Select two or more tasks. 2. Press Ctrl + I , or choose Inspector in the selection bar. 3. Set Start , Estimate or Flag Flag all or Unflag . A field that differs between the tasks says Mixed ; Clear removes it from all of them. Plan my day with estimates and a focus block Plan my day guide:plan lays your day out as time blocks. With Power tools on: - A task's estimate is its length in the plan, so the meter shows how full your day really is. - The plan starts with a focus block of 60 to 90 minutes \"for the work that moves your top initiatives\": the available tasks of your highest-ranked initiatives guide:pro/portfolio . Keep it, or untick its tasks like any other. The weekly review, for execution The weekly review guide:review gets five steps before its usual ones, each with a count: Step What it is about --- --- Give verdicts Promises others made you that are due: kept, not yet, or another verdict Send what was agreed What was agreed in a meeting and not sent in a recap yet Escalations and decisions Escalations drafted or on the agenda, and decisions you are owed Check the portfolio Initiatives not ready, over the cap or without an available task Note the value delivered Initiatives closed this week without the value they delivered Each step has Open to go there and Mark done . The mirror is one line about your last seven days, for example: \"Last 7 days: you took over 1 promise and had a focus block on 3 of 5 workdays. You hold 42 open tasks; others hold 6 promises to you.\" It is a reflection, not a score. Look back on a quarter On Portfolio , choose Quarterly impact for a summary of This quarter or Last quarter : the value delivered, the initiatives closed, the decisions made and how promises went. Add a Growth note about your own quarter; it appears only in the copy. Copy it for a performance review or a report, or print it. Related - What Pro is guide:pro - Custom lists guide:custom-lists - Plan your day guide:plan - Review and guided organizing guide:review - Keyboard shortcuts guide:shortcuts"},{"s":"plan","t":"Plan your day","d":"Give tasks a time with time blocks, use the Plan screen and its timeline, and let Plan my day arrange a day from what is on your list.","h":[["in-short","In short"],["time-blocks","Time blocks"],["make-a-block","Make a block"],["what-a-block-does","What a block does"],["turn-on-the-plan-screen","Turn on the Plan screen"],["use-the-timeline","Use the timeline"],["place-a-task-on-a-time","Place a task on a time"],["move-resize-and-draw","Move, resize and draw"],["move-a-block-to-another-day","Move a block to another day"],["the-row-above-the-timeline","The row above the timeline"],["the-left-panel","The left panel"],["routines-and-due-times","Routines and due times"],["the-gear","The gear"],["use-the-timeline-2","Use the timeline"],["plan-my-day","Plan my day"],["with-the-assistant","With the assistant"],["details","Details"],["desktop-and-phone","Desktop and phone"],["related","Related"]],"x":"In short - A time block is a task with a start and an end time. Type Call Sam tomorrow at 3pm for 30m anywhere you add a task. Blocks work on every device, even with the Plan screen off. - The Plan screen is optional. It is off until you turn it on, per device. It shows a day timeline where you move, stretch, draw and drop blocks. - The due date still says which day a task belongs to. The block adds what time you set aside for it. - Plan my day is a four-step session that takes leftovers from yesterday, lets you pick what to do, arranges it in your free time and writes it only when you accept. - Nothing here needs the assistant. With the assistant set up, Plan my day can also suggest lengths and an order. Plan's settings sit with Vicu Pro's tools, but Plan does not need Pro: it works the same whether Pro is on or off. With Pro's Power tools guide:pro/power on, Plan my day also uses your estimates and proposes a focus block. Plan's settings sit with Vicu Pro's tools, but Plan does not need Pro: it works the same whether Pro is on or off. With Pro's Power tools guide:pro/power on, Plan my day also uses your estimates and proposes a focus block. Time blocks A block is the task's own start and end time. Vikunja stores it as the task's start date and end date, so it shows in the Vikunja web app and on every device. A task without a block is a complete task, and nothing moves on its own. Make a block Write the length after a time when you add a task, in the new-task row, in Quick Entry, in pasted lines, by voice or in the phone's quick add: - Call Sam tomorrow at 3pm for 30m - Write report friday at 9am for 2 hours You need a time of day. for 30m on its own stays in the title. More about this in Quick add guide:quick-add . What a block does - A task whose block starts today is on Today, even if its due date is later. - The row shows the block, for example \"15:00 to 15:30\". A date-only due date on the same day is left out, because the block already says the day. - Change the due date and a block on that day moves with it. Clear the due date and the time goes too. - A copy of a task keeps its block. - A default reminder, if you set one, goes by the block's start. Local tasks in standalone mode on the desktop have only a due date, so they have no time blocks. Local tasks in standalone mode on the desktop have only a due date, so they have no time blocks. Turn on the Plan screen The setting is per device. The things you set in the gear default length, work hours follow you to your other devices. Use the timeline The Plan screen has a switch for Day , 3 days and Week . The week starts on Monday. The list of tasks sits on the left and the timeline on the right. Place a task on a time - Drag a task from the list onto a time. It takes the default length: 30 minutes to start, changeable in the gear. - If the task is due before that time, its due date moves to that day and a message says so, with Undo. A later due date stays as a deadline. - Fit today in the gear gives the day's tasks without a time the next free slots, most urgent first, with Undo. Move, resize and draw To do this Do this --- --- Move a block Drag it. It moves in 15-minute steps. Change the start or the end Drag the top or bottom edge. Set an exact length Click the block and pick a length. Add a new task Drag on an empty time. Type a title. It goes into the project you are browsing, or the Inbox. Add a task at the next free time Press Ctrl + N Cmd on a Mac . Remove the time Drag the block back onto the list. The date stays. Move it by keyboard Alt + Shift + Up or Down moves a focused block 15 minutes. Shift + Up or Down changes its length. Enter opens the lengths. Pressing Escape or leaving the window while you drag cancels the change. The block is written when you let go. Move a block to another day Click a block and use its day row: Today, Tomorrow, the next few days, or Pick a date . The block keeps its times, the view goes to that day, and there is an Undo. Past days cannot be picked. In Day view you can also drag a block or a task onto Back or Forward and hold it there for half a second to turn to the next day. Keep holding to go further, then drop on a time. Back stops at today. The row above the timeline Tasks due on a day without a time sit in a row above that day's timeline. While you drag a block, the row shows \"Drop here to remove the time\". The left panel Chips choose where the list takes its tasks from. Each shows a count once its list is loaded. - The selected day without a time \"Today\" - Inbox - Next 7 days - No date - Project , which searches every project The box under the chips filters by title. Routines and due times Routines show as small pills at their times. They have no length and never count as busy. Turn them off with Show routines on the timeline in the gear. Tasks with a due time also get a small marker on the timeline. The gear The gear Plan settings holds the default length, your work hours 08:00 to 18:00 to start , Ask me to plan tomorrow , Show routines on the timeline and your calendars see Events and calendars guide:calendars . Work hours and default length sync to your other devices. Free time is your work hours less what is already planned. Use the timeline The phone shows one day at a time, with a strip of seven days above it. The phone shows one day at a time, with a strip of seven days above it. - Swipe the timeline sideways for the next or previous day. Past the end of the strip, the strip moves a week. A swipe on the strip itself moves a week. - No time is the row above the hours. It holds the day's tasks without a time. Tap one and then a time to place it, press and hold to drag it, or tick it. - The tab at the right edge opens the tasks to plan, with the same chips as the desktop. Tap a task and then a time, or press and hold and drag it onto the timeline. - Press and hold a block to move it. Drag its handle to change the length. Both go in 15-minute steps. - Tap a block for another day, exact lengths and Remove time . - Another day while you drag: rest the block on a day of the strip for half a second, or tap the day with a second finger. Let go on the strip day to keep the same time on that day. Let go on No time to remove the time. - Press and hold an empty time to add a task or an event. - Today has a List and a Day switch while the planner is on. Day shows the timeline and lets you open and tick tasks. Changing them happens on Plan. - Under the days of the strip, small load bars show how full each day is. The gear has the default length, work hours, the evening nudge and the routine pills. It also lists your events and has New event . Plan my day Plan my day is a session for today or tomorrow. It writes nothing until you accept. On the desktop it opens from the Plan screen the Plan my day button . On the phone it is the sun button on Plan. On the last step: - Undo all puts back everything the session changed. - Remind me when each block starts adds a reminder to each block. - Ask me to plan tomorrow sends one notification each evening at a time you set, on this device. Tapping it opens Plan. You can also set it in the gear or in Settings, Pro, Plan . Events and connected calendars count as busy time, so the draft keeps clear of your meetings. See Events and calendars guide:calendars . With the assistant With the assistant set up see The assistant guide:assistant , a switch in the session adds suggestions. Each is dashed with a short reason, and nothing is saved until you accept. - Leftovers: one of the four choices for each, with Apply the suggestions . - Choose: how long each task takes, tasks worth adding, and what can wait when you are over your free time. - Arrange: an order with start times, long work in long gaps and quick tasks in short ones. First free slots is one tap away. It sends one request when the session opens, and another when you change the day or press Ask again. The request holds each task's title, project, priority, due date, subtask count and the start of its notes, plus your free time and your routines' times without their names. If it fails, the session says so once and carries on with first free slots and your default length. The assistant never sees event titles, only the free time. With Pro guide:pro/power on, estimates become the lengths, and the session can propose a daily focus block for tasks of your top initiatives. With Pro guide:pro/power on, estimates become the lengths, and the session can propose a daily focus block for tasks of your top initiatives. Details - Where times show. Blocks show everywhere. Events and calendars show only on Plan and in Plan my day, not on Today, in lists or in reminders. - Past and busy. Timed, busy events count as busy for free time, Fit, the phone's load bars and Plan my day. All-day and free events do not. Desktop and phone Desktop Phone --- --- --- Views Day, 3 days, Week One day with a strip of seven days Place a task Drag from the list Tap a task then a time, or press and hold and drag Fit Fit today in the gear Next free Draw Drag on an empty time Press and hold an empty time Keyboard Arrow keys move and resize Not applicable Plan my day Button on Plan Sun button on Plan Evening nudge Notification on this device Notification on this device Related - Events and calendars guide:calendars - Quick add guide:quick-add - Routines guide:routines - The assistant guide:assistant - Keyboard shortcuts guide:shortcuts"},{"s":"calendars","t":"Events and calendars","d":"Add your own repeating Events and show your work and personal calendars on the Plan screen, so meetings count as busy time when you plan.","h":[["in-short","In short"],["your-own-events","Your own Events"],["add-an-event","Add an Event"],["move-and-resize","Move and resize"],["change-a-repeating-event","Change a repeating Event"],["delete-an-event","Delete an Event"],["connected-calendars","Connected calendars"],["how-to-add-one","How to add one"],["a-calendar-file-on-this-computer","A calendar file on this computer"],["classic-outlook-on-windows","Classic Outlook on Windows"],["microsoft-365-and-google","Microsoft 365 and Google"],["settings-for-each-calendar","Settings for each calendar"],["how-often-calendars-are-read","How often calendars are read"],["phone-calendars","Phone calendars"],["import-a-calendar-file","Import a calendar file"],["privacy-and-security","Privacy and security"],["for-it-administrators","For IT administrators"],["related","Related"]],"x":"In short - Events are your own entries on the Plan screen: a weekly manager call, a month-end close. They repeat if you want, and they sync to all your devices through your Vikunja server. - Connected calendars show other calendars work, personal, family on the Plan screen. They are read only: Vicu never changes them. - Both count as busy time , so free time, Fit and Plan my day keep clear of them. - They show on Plan only , not on Today, in lists or in reminders. - Calendar data stays on the device. It is never sent to your Vikunja server, your other devices or the assistant. The assistant only gets free time ranges, never an event's title. - You need the Plan screen guide:plan turned on to see any of this. Your own Events An Event is not a task. It has nothing to tick off. It says \"I am not available then\". Events are saved as part of your synced settings, so they appear on every device where Plan is on. Add an Event Drag on an empty time on the timeline and pick Event , or choose New event in the command palette, or press Ctrl + Shift + N Cmd on a Mac while on Plan. The title box understands quick-add text. Drag on an empty time on the timeline and pick Event , or choose New event in the command palette, or press Ctrl + Shift + N Cmd on a Mac while on Plan. The title box understands quick-add text. Press and hold an empty time on the timeline and choose Event . The gear also lists your Events and has New event . Press and hold an empty time on the timeline and choose Event . The gear also lists your Events and has New event . An Event has a title, All day or a start time and length, how it repeats, when it ends Never or On a date and Counts as busy time . The repeat list offers choices that fit the day you pressed, such as \"every week on Tuesday\". Move and resize Events move and resize like blocks: drag, drag an edge, or use the keyboard Alt + Shift + Up or Down to move, Shift + Up or Down to change length on the desktop. On the phone, press and hold to move and drag the handle to change length. Change a repeating Event When an Event repeats, Vicu asks which times your change is for: - This time only - This and following - All times On the desktop the question appears in a small menu after you let go of a drag, and the Event's popover has a Change selector at the top for the same choice. On the phone it is asked when you save. Skip this one and Delete are in the Event too, and each has Undo. If you change the repeat or the end, it applies to this and following times or to all times, not to a single time. If you change the repeat or the end, it applies to this and following times or to all times, not to a single time. Delete an Event Open the Event and choose Delete event on the phone, Delete . On the desktop you can also delete from Settings, Pro, Plan where your Events are listed. Undo brings it back. Connected calendars How to add one The kinds you can add on the desktop: Kind Good for What you do --- --- --- Calendar link Any calendar with a secret iCal .ics address Paste the link, name it, choose Add calendar . The dialog says where Google, Outlook, iCloud and Proton keep that address. CalDAV account iCloud, Fastmail, Nextcloud and other CalDAV servers Enter the Server address and an App password , sign in, pick which calendars to show. Microsoft 365 Outlook.com and work or school accounts Sign in in your browser, then pick calendars. Google Google Calendar, personal or Workspace Sign in in your browser, then pick calendars. Mac calendars The calendars in macOS Calendar macOS asks once whether Vicu may read them. No sign-in in Vicu. Classic Outlook The calendars of classic Outlook on a Windows PC Read the warnings, tick that you may show them, then pick calendars. Calendar file An .ics file another program keeps up to date Name it and choose the file. Mac calendars is offered on macOS only. It lists everything you added in System Settings, Internet Accounts, and picks up a change at once. A calendar file on this computer Any program that can write an .ics file can feed Plan: Thunderbird, a script, a scheduled export. Choose Calendar file , give it a name and choose the file. Vicu reads it again as soon as the file changes, and never writes to it. - Keep the file where it is. If it moves or is deleted, the calendar keeps its last copy and says the file is no longer there. Remove the calendar and add the file again from its new place. - A file is read whole, up to 15 MB. - To copy a file's events into your own Events once instead, use Import a calendar file import-a-calendar-file . Classic Outlook on Windows Classic Outlook is offered on Windows only. It reads the calendars of the desktop Outlook on this PC, and the data stays on the PC. Before you add it: - Only classic Outlook can be read, and only while it is open. Vicu never starts it. The new Outlook has no way for other apps to read it: add that account as Microsoft 365 or a Calendar link instead. - Outlook may ask whether to allow a program to access its data. Vicu reads only titles, places, times, \"show as\" and how you answered, not people or messages. - On a work PC, this shows your organisation's calendar in Vicu. Check that your organisation allows it. The form asks you to tick These are my calendars, and I may show them in Vicu on this PC before it reads Outlook. While Outlook is closed, the calendars keep their last copy and say \"Open classic Outlook to read its calendars\". Microsoft 365 and Google Vicu asks for read-only access. For Microsoft 365 it uses the permission that reads your calendar list and events, and for Google two read-only permissions. Vicu cannot create, change or delete anything and cannot answer invitations. If your organization does not let people approve apps themselves, you see \"Your organisation needs to approve Vicu\". Send your IT team the page for them: Vicu and your organisation's calendars https://github.com/rendyhd/Vicu/blob/main/docs/calendar-it-admins.md . It lists the permissions and how an administrator can approve Vicu for everyone. The same button is in the sign-in form as The page for your IT team . If sign-in stops working, a Sign in again button appears on the account. Advanced: use my own client lets you register your own app with Microsoft or Google and use that instead of Vicu's. Settings for each calendar Each calendar has: - A checkbox to show or hide it on Plan - A colour - Counts as busy time - Show only \"Busy\", not titles : Vicu then keeps no titles or places for that calendar at all, only that you are busy Timed, busy events of calendars that count are busy for free time, Fit, the load bars on the phone and Plan my day. All-day and free events are not busy. How often calendars are read Vicu reads calendars when you open Plan and again when the window comes to the front, but at most every 5 minutes. A copy of the last read is kept so Plan works offline. If a calendar cannot be read, it keeps the last good copy and says \"Not updated since\" with the time. To read at once on the desktop, press F5 or Ctrl + R Cmd + R on a Mac , choose Refresh in the command palette, or use the Read the calendars again button in Settings. On the phone, pull down on the timeline. Phone calendars The phone does not add accounts of its own. In the Plan gear, choose Calendars . Vicu asks to read your calendars and lists the ones the phone already has, grouped by account. - On Android that is what the phone syncs: Google, Exchange through Outlook, iCloud or other CalDAV through DAVx5, subscribed links through ICSx5. - On an iPhone or iPad it is the accounts in Settings, Calendar: iCloud, Google, Exchange, CalDAV and subscriptions. Access is asked once and is full access, because the system has no read-only choice. Each calendar can be shown or hidden, take a colour, count as busy or not, and show only Busy. Vicu never writes to these calendars. If you refuse access, your own Events still work. The phone does not add accounts of its own. In the Plan gear, choose Calendars . Vicu asks to read your calendars and lists the ones the phone already has, grouped by account. - On Android that is what the phone syncs: Google, Exchange through Outlook, iCloud or other CalDAV through DAVx5, subscribed links through ICSx5. - On an iPhone or iPad it is the accounts in Settings, Calendar: iCloud, Google, Exchange, CalDAV and subscriptions. Access is asked once and is full access, because the system has no read-only choice. Each calendar can be shown or hidden, take a colour, count as busy or not, and show only Busy. Vicu never writes to these calendars. If you refuse access, your own Events still work. Import a calendar file You can turn an .ics file into your own Events. This suits a school timetable, public holidays or an exported work schedule. Rules for the import: - A series Vicu can repeat daily, weekly, monthly on a date or weekday, yearly, with its end and skipped days becomes one repeating Event. - Anything else becomes single Events. On the phone, that means the next 12 months. - Times with a time zone become your local time. - The desktop imports up to 500 Events at a time, and leaves out Events that ended before today, cancelled ones and those more than a year ahead. - Afterwards the Events are yours. They sync, you can change them, and nothing stays linked to the file. Privacy and security - Calendars are read on this device only. They are never sent to your Vikunja server, to your other devices or to the assistant. - On the desktop, sign-in tokens and the saved copy are encrypted with your system's protection. On a Linux system without a keyring, Settings warns that links and passwords are only obscured. - Removing a calendar account in Settings deletes its tokens and copy from the device. - Your own Events are different: they are part of your synced settings on your Vikunja server, like your custom lists. For IT administrators Work calendars often need an administrator to approve an app. The page Vicu and your organisation's calendars https://github.com/rendyhd/Vicu/blob/main/docs/calendar-it-admins.md explains what Vicu asks for, where the data goes, and how to grant consent for the whole organization in Microsoft 365 and Google Workspace. Related - Plan your day guide:plan - Routines guide:routines - The assistant guide:assistant - Settings guide:settings"},{"s":"quick-add","t":"Quick add syntax","d":"Every word Vicu reads in a task's title: dates, times, repeats, time blocks, projects, labels, priority and places, with examples of what each one produces.","h":[["in-short","In short"],["dates","Dates"],["times","Times"],["what-vicu-does-not-read-as-a-date","What Vicu does not read as a date"],["more-than-one-date","More than one date"],["words-that-go-with-a-date","Words that go with a date"],["due-today-with-an-exclamation-mark","Due today with an exclamation mark"],["repeats","Repeats"],["time-blocks","Time blocks"],["projects-and-labels","Projects and labels"],["priority","Priority"],["places","Places"],["pro-phrases","Pro phrases"],["examples","Examples"],["where-it-works","Where it works"],["related","Related"]],"x":"In short - Type naturally. Call Ana tomorrow 3pm work @call p2 makes a task called \"Call Ana\", due tomorrow at 3 PM, in the Work project, with the label call and high priority. - Everything Vicu reads is highlighted and shown as a chip. Remove a chip and that text stays in the title. The rest of what you typed is the title. - The same rules everywhere. The desktop, Android and iPhone read titles identically. It works in the new-task row, Quick Entry, pasted lines, voice and the phone's quick add sheet. - Pick your style. Todoist style project , @label , p1 is the default. Vikunja style +project , label , !1 is in Settings, General, Task Parser on the desktop and Settings, General, Input Parsing on the phone. Parsing can be turned off there too. - Suggestions appear as you type. After or @ or + or in Vikunja style , Vicu lists your projects or labels. Dates A date can be anywhere in the title. You type What you get --- --- today , tomorrow That day friday , this friday The next Friday, today included next friday Friday of next week. On a Tuesday, next friday is the Friday after this one. friday next week The same as next friday next week The Monday of next week next month The same day next month in 3 days , in 2 weeks That long from today jan 15 , 15 jan , march 3rd That date. If it already passed this year, it means next year. 10/15 In your system's order: month first for US English, day first for most other regions. If the first number cannot be a month, the order flips. 2026-10-15 That date, unambiguously A date without a time is due at the end of that day and shows without a time. Times You type What you get --- --- 3pm , 3:30pm , 3 p.m. That time at 14:00 That time tomorrow at 3pm , friday 3pm , jan 15 9:30am The date with the time in 2 hours , in 30 minutes That long from now, to the minute A time with no date means today if it is still ahead, otherwise tomorrow. What Vicu does not read as a date These stay in the title, so a task keeps its words: - weekend and weekday : \"Plan the weekend\" has no date. - The past: yesterday , last friday , last week , 2 days ago . - An amount of time on its own: \"Call 30m\" or \"Rest 3 days\". An amount counts only right after in . - Three-letter weekdays by themselves: \"Buy sun cream\" has no date. mon , tue , wed , thu , fri , sat and sun count after on , next , this , by or due , or with a time wed 3pm . Full names always count. - A date inside another word or bracket: \"Monday's report\", \" tomorrow \", \"Plan Friday/Saturday\". - now . More than one date - The first date is the due date. \"Book Friday dinner tomorrow\" is due Friday. - A weekday right before another date stays in the title: \"Call Ana about Saturday tomorrow at 3pm\" is due tomorrow at 3 PM. - There are no ranges. \"Trip friday to sunday\" is due Friday, and the title is \"Trip to sunday\". - A label, project or priority in the middle of a date splits it: \"Call tomorrow @home 3pm\" is due tomorrow with the title \"Call 3pm\". Words that go with a date on , by and due before a date, and at before a time, leave with the date. \"Submit report by friday\" becomes \"Submit report\", and \"Taxes due tomorrow\" becomes \"Taxes\". Due today with an exclamation mark A lone ! at the start or end makes a task due today: Buy milk ! or ! buy milk . A ! inside the text \"Hello! world\" does nothing. It works even when parsing is turned off. On the desktop you can turn it off in Settings, General, Task Parser . Repeats You type What you get --- --- every day , daily Every day every week , weekly Every week every month , monthly Every month every year , yearly , annually Every year every 3 days , every 2 weeks Every N days, weeks, months or years biweekly , fortnightly Every 2 weeks every monday Every week, and the due date is the next Monday. A time after it applies: every monday 10am . daily , weekly and the other one-word forms count only as the last word. \"Water plants daily\" repeats. \"Weekly standup\" and \"Daily review tomorrow\" do not, because the word describes the task. If you also give a date, it is the first due date: every friday starting oct 20 starts on October 20. Time blocks for and a length, together with a time of day, set aside that time for the task. You type What you get --- --- Call Sam tomorrow at 3pm for 30m A block tomorrow from 3:00 to 3:30 PM Write report at 9am for 2 hours Today from 9 to 11 AM, or tomorrow if 9 AM has passed Review at 10am for 1h30m From 10:00 to 11:30 AM - Lengths are 30m , 90 min , 2 hours , 1h30m or 1h 30m , from 1 minute to 12 hours. - Without a time of day, the words stay in the title: \"Call tomorrow for 30m\" is due tomorrow with the title \"Call tomorrow for 30m\". \"Gift for mum\" and \"Table for 4\" are never read as lengths. - for 3 days and for 3 months are never dates. See Plan your day guide:plan for what a time block does. Projects and labels Style Project Label --- --- --- Todoist default work , \"side project\" @errand , @\"grocery list\" Vikunja +work , +\"side project\" errand , \"grocery list\" - Put a name with spaces in double quotes. - A project name starts with a letter, so review PR 142 keeps \" 142\" in the title. - Vicu does not check that the project or label exists. Pick from the suggestions to be sure. - In Vikunja style, @john stays in the title. Priority Priority Todoist style Vikunja style Words both styles --- --- --- --- Urgent p1 !4 !urgent , !critical High p2 !3 !high Medium p3 !2 !medium , !med Low p4 !1 !low p1 is the most urgent in Todoist style, as in Todoist. A word such as p2p is not a priority. Places If you saved places on your phone, a phrase that names one sets a location reminder. You type What you get --- --- Call mom when I get home Title \"Call mom\", with a reminder when you arrive at Home Buy stamps when I leave work A reminder when you leave Work When I'm at the office, send the report With a saved place called Office: title \"send the report\", reminder on arriving there - Arriving phrases include when I get , when I arrive at , when I'm at , when I get back to , once I get , arriving at . Leaving phrases include when I leave , when I'm leaving , once I leave and leaving . - Vicu reads it only for a place you saved, and only whole words: \"when I get homework done\" names no place. - The reminder is created once the task reaches the server. A task created offline on the desktop gets none, and the composer tells you so. See Vicu on your phone guide:phone location-reminders-and-places . Pro phrases When Pro guide:pro is on, a few more words are read. With Pro off they stay in the title. You type What you get --- --- Jan: send the deck friday A promise from Jan to you. Jan must be someone you marked as a person. for Jan: review the budget Your promise to Jan starts monday A start date. The task stays out of Available until then. ~30m , ~1h30 , ~45min An estimate of 30 minutes, 1 hour 30 minutes or 45 minutes !flag Flags the task req: can you add a report Marks a request for triage. The triage tools are on the desktop. Names for people are matched by their name, a name they go by, or an unambiguous first name. An unknown or ambiguous name stays in the title. See Promises and people guide:pro/promises . Examples You type The task --- --- Buy groceries tomorrow 3pm shopping @errands p3 \"Buy groceries\", due tomorrow 3 PM, project Shopping, label errands, medium priority Submit report by fri \"Submit report\", due Friday Weekly standup every monday 10am @work \"Weekly standup\", repeats weekly, next Monday at 10:00, label work Water plants daily \"Water plants\", repeats every day Call Sam tomorrow at 3pm for 30m \"Call Sam\", a block tomorrow from 3:00 to 3:30 PM Dentist jan 15 9:30am \"Dentist\", due January 15 at 9:30 AM Call dentist ! \"Call dentist\", due today Pay rent in 3 days \"Pay rent\", due 3 days from now Review PR 142 The title is kept whole, with no project Plan the weekend No date: \"weekend\" is not a date Notes from last friday No date: the past is not read Trip friday to sunday \"Trip to sunday\", due Friday Call mom when I get home \"Call mom\", with a location reminder at Home Task +inbox urgent !4 In Vikunja style: \"Task\", project inbox, label urgent, urgent priority Where it works Quick add text is read in the new-task row on every list, the title editor, Quick Entry guide:desktop quick-entry , pasted lines one task per line , voice, and the phone's quick add sheet. On the phone, typed words move into chips as you type. On iOS, a title shared from another app is not parsed. Related - Add tasks guide:capture - Plan your day guide:plan - Vicu Pro guide:pro - Settings guide:settings - Keyboard shortcuts guide:shortcuts"},{"s":"shortcuts","t":"Keyboard shortcuts","d":"The complete list of keyboard shortcuts in the Vicu desktop app, grouped by where you use them, with the few that you can change.","h":[["in-short","In short"],["shortcuts-you-can-change","Shortcuts you can change"],["everywhere-in-the-app","Everywhere in the app"],["moving-through-a-list","Moving through a list"],["working-on-tasks","Working on tasks"],["in-an-open-task","In an open task"],["projects-in-the-sidebar","Projects in the sidebar"],["plan","Plan"],["ask-and-comments","Ask and comments"],["guided-sessions","Guided sessions"],["quick-entry-and-quick-view","Quick Entry and Quick View"],["pro","Pro"],["related","Related"]],"x":"In short - Keys are written for Windows and Linux. On a Mac, use Cmd where this page says Ctrl , and Option where it says Alt . - Three shortcuts can be changed: Quick Entry, Quick View and the voice shortcut, in Settings . The rest are fixed. - The two shortcuts that work in any app are Quick Entry and Quick View, after you turn them on. - A shortcut that does nothing is usually in the wrong place. For example, the list shortcuts need the list to have focus, and the Pro shortcuts need Pro to be on. - Settings, Keyboard Shortcuts shows the main shortcuts inside the app. Shortcuts you can change Shortcut Default Where to change it --- --- --- Quick Entry Alt + Shift + V , or Cmd + Shift + Space on a Mac Settings, Quick Entry / View . Click the hotkey and press the new one. Quick View Alt + Shift + B , or Cmd + Shift + B on a Mac Settings, Quick Entry / View Voice input None. It stays off until you record one. Settings, Keyboard Shortcuts, Voice input . It works only while Vicu is in front. Both Quick shortcuts work from any app and must be turned on first. See On the desktop guide:desktop . Everywhere in the app Keys What it does --- --- Ctrl + Shift + P Open the command palette Ctrl + F Go to Quick find Ctrl + N New task File, New task Ctrl + J Show or hide Ask Ctrl + Shift + C Show or hide comments Ctrl + P Print the list File, Print F5 or Ctrl + R Refresh from the server View, Refresh Ctrl + Shift + R Reload the window View, Reload window Cmd + , Open Settings macOS app menu The View menu also has zoom in, zoom out, reset zoom and full screen. Moving through a list Keys What it does --- --- Up and Down Previous and next task Enter Open the task Esc Close the task, clear focus, or clear the selection Space Complete the focused task and move to the next Ctrl + A Select all tasks Delete or Backspace Delete the selected tasks. It asks first if Confirm before deleting is on. Alt + Shift + Up or Down Move the task up or down a manual order, or among tasks without a date Working on tasks Keys What it does --- --- Ctrl + K Complete the selected task. In an open task, complete and close it. Ctrl + T Set the due date to today Ctrl + D Duplicate the selected tasks Ctrl + C Copy the selected tasks' titles Ctrl + V Make tasks from the clipboard, one task per line ! Due today. Type it in a title, or on a list. In an open task Keys What it does --- --- Enter in the title Move to the notes Esc Save and close Ctrl + Enter Save and close Ctrl + K Complete and close Ctrl + Shift + C Show or hide comments Ctrl + Shift + V in notes Paste without turning Markdown into formatting Alt + Up or Down in a notes list Move the line up or down Left and Right image viewer Previous and next image Projects in the sidebar Keys What it does --- --- F2 Rename the focused project Shift + F10 or the Menu key Open its menu Alt + Shift +arrow keys Move the project up or down, into the one above, or out one level Plan Keys What it does --- --- Ctrl + N New task at the day's next free time Ctrl + Shift + N New event Alt + Shift + Up or Down Move a block or your event 15 minutes earlier or later Shift + Up or Down Make it 15 minutes shorter or longer Enter Open the block's lengths See Plan your day guide:plan . Ask and comments Keys What it does --- --- Ctrl + J Show or hide Ask Enter in Ask Send the message Ctrl + Enter in the text box for making tasks Make tasks from the text Ctrl + Enter in comments Post the comment @ in comments Mention someone Esc in comments Close comments See The assistant guide:assistant and Work with others guide:collaboration . Guided sessions In a card session such as Sort the Inbox : Keys What it does --- --- Enter or Right Next. Saves the card's choices. Left Back S Skip 1 to 4 The quick dates: Today, Tomorrow, This weekend, Next week D Pick a date 0 No date P Pick a project E or F2 Edit the title C Complete Delete Delete Esc Leave the session On a project review page, R marks the project reviewed and S skips it. See Review and guided organizing guide:review . Quick Entry and Quick View These keys work inside their small windows. Keys What it does --- --- Enter Quick Entry Save the task Tab Quick Entry Add a description Ctrl + L Quick Entry Link the browser tab or Obsidian note Ctrl + Left or Right Quick Entry Switch project. The modifier is a setting. Up , Down , Enter Quick View Move, and complete the selected task Tab , Shift + Enter , ! Quick View Show the description, edit, set due today Esc Close the window Pro These work only while Pro guide:pro is on. Keys What it does --- --- Ctrl + Shift + F Flag or unflag the selected tasks Ctrl + Shift + O Focus on some projects Ctrl + Shift + M Import meeting notes Ctrl + I Open the inspector for two or more selected tasks 1 to 6 on a promise row Delivered, Late, Not yet, New date, Dropped, Ask me tomorrow Enter , S , A in a meeting round Next person, skip, absent Ctrl + Shift + I stays the developer tools. See Promises and people guide:pro/promises and Power tools guide:pro/power . Related - On the desktop guide:desktop - Settings guide:settings - Quick add guide:quick-add - Vicu Pro guide:pro"},{"s":"faq","t":"Troubleshooting and FAQ","d":"Answers to common Vicu problems: sign-in and passkeys, API token permissions, sync and offline, tasks that seem to have vanished, Linux, macOS and Windows notes.","h":[["in-short","In short"],["sign-in-and-connecting","Sign-in and connecting"],["which-vikunja-version-do-i-need","Which Vikunja version do I need?"],["single-sign-on-shows-an-endless-spinner-or-an-unknown-error-occurred","Single sign-on shows an endless spinner or \"An unknown error occurred\""],["my-provider-refuses-to-sign-in-on-iphone","My provider refuses to sign in on iPhone"],["session-expired-or-the-app-asks-me-to-sign-in-again","\"Session expired\", or the app asks me to sign in again"],["i-use-an-api-token-and-some-things-fail","I use an API token and some things fail"],["vicu-warns-about-an-http-address","Vicu warns about an http:// address"],["sync-and-offline","Sync and offline"],["my-changes-do-not-show-on-my-other-device","My changes do not show on my other device"],["a-change-failed-and-i-do-not-know-why","A change failed and I do not know why"],["does-vicu-work-offline","Does Vicu work offline?"],["my-custom-lists-or-settings-are-not-on-the-new-device","My custom lists or settings are not on the new device"],["my-task-disappeared","My task disappeared"],["why-do-i-see-strange-tasks-in-the-vikunja-web-app","Why do I see strange tasks in the Vikunja web app?"],["linux","Linux"],["macos","macOS"],["windows","Windows"],["where-is-my-data","Where is my data?"],["report-a-bug","Report a bug"],["related","Related"]],"x":"In short - Sign-in trouble: check that your server runs Vikunja 2.4.0 or newer, then see the sign-in section below. - Changes not showing on another device: refresh F5 on the desktop, pull down on the phone and look at the sync status. - A task seems gone: it is probably in the Logbook, hidden by a filter, or waiting for a start date. See \"My task disappeared\". - Still stuck? Open an issue https://github.com/rendyhd/Vicu/issues and say what you did and what you saw. Sign-in and connecting Which Vikunja version do I need? Vikunja 2.4.0 or newer, on your own server or on Vikunja Cloud. Vicu has no server or account of its own. Single sign-on shows an endless spinner or \"An unknown error occurred\" Your identity provider probably asks for a passkey Pocket ID does, for example . Passkeys cannot complete inside Vicu's own sign-in window on macOS and Linux, and in the iPhone and Android in-app sign-in. 1. On the sign-in screen, choose Uses a passkey? Sign in through your browser . Vicu also offers this by itself when it notices a passkey prompt. 2. Copy the redirect address Vicu shows, using its Copy button. 3. Add it once as a redirect callback URL of the Vikunja client at your identity provider. On the desktop the address is http://127.0.0.1:47823/vicu/oidc-callback . On the phone it is com.rendyhd.vicu:/oidc-callback . 4. Start the browser sign-in again. Vicu remembers which way you signed in and uses it again when you have to sign in again. On Windows the desktop app is not affected, because Windows Hello handles passkeys. My provider refuses to sign in on iPhone Some providers, Google among them, refuse to sign in inside an app's web view. Use Sign in through your browser , a username and password, or an API token. \"Session expired\", or the app asks me to sign in again Your sign-in ran out. Sign in again. Waiting changes are kept and sent afterwards. See Sync and offline guide:sync . I use an API token and some things fail Vikunja answers 401 when a token lacks a permission group. Vicu tells you which one is missing instead of saying the session expired. A token needs these permissions: Permission group Used for --- --- tasks , projects , labels Reading, creating, changing and deleting them task_labels Adding and removing labels task_relations Subtasks and relations task_attachments Files on tasks project_views Task position and sorting For collaboration guide:collaboration , the token also needs task comments, task assignees and project sharing. Without them Vicu says what is missing. Changes the token may not send go to the failed list, and the rest continue. Password and single sign-on logins have full access, so they avoid all of this. Following a task for comments \"Notify me\" works only with those logins. On the desktop, a small i next to the token field lists the permissions. Vicu warns about an http:// address An http:// address outside your own network sends your sign-in unencrypted. Use https:// where you can. A server with a certificate from your own certificate authority works on Android once that certificate is installed on the phone. Sync and offline My changes do not show on my other device 1. Refresh. F5 on the desktop, pull down on a list on the phone. 2. Check the sync status. On the desktop a button appears at the bottom of the sidebar if something waits or failed. On the phone see Settings, General, Data & Sync . 3. Open the panel and Retry all if something failed. A change failed and I do not know why The sync panel gives a reason beside each failed change: the task was deleted elsewhere, a file is too large, you may no longer edit that project, or your token is not allowed. Fix the cause, then Retry , or Discard the change. See Sync and offline guide:sync the-sync-status-button . Does Vicu work offline? Yes. You can add, edit, complete and delete tasks. They wait and go out in order when the connection returns. Sharing, uploading a file to a comment and some creating of projects need a connection. My custom lists or settings are not on the new device They travel through your server in the background. Make sure the old device has synced it may show \"Lists and settings\" in the sync panel , then refresh the new one. See Settings guide:settings for what syncs. My task disappeared Nothing in Vicu deletes a task by itself. Check these first. - You just completed it. A completed task stays in the list for about 5 seconds, then collapses, and a Completed message with Undo appears. Finished tasks live in the Logbook . On the phone, Settings, General, Logbook can hide older completed tasks, but it never deletes them. - It is not due yet. Today shows what is due today and overdue. Upcoming starts tomorrow. A task with no date is in Anytime, or in the Inbox. - A filter hides it. Search has filter chips for date, status, labels and priority. A custom list shows only what matches its conditions. - Its project is archived. Archived projects are collapsed under Archived in the sidebar. Restore the project to bring it back. - Pro is on and it has a start date. Lists can show Available , hiding tasks that start later or wait on others. Use Show or switch the list to All . See Know what you can do now guide:pro/available . - It is not a task at all. Vicu keeps custom lists, routines, synced settings and Pro records in hidden tasks. Vicu hides them everywhere, and you may see them in the Vikunja web app. Leave them there. Why do I see strange tasks in the Vikunja web app? Those are Vicu's hidden tasks, such as \"Vicu preferences sync metadata — do not delete \". Do not delete or edit them. Deleting one deletes the data it holds. Linux - Global shortcuts do nothing on Wayland. Accept the GlobalShortcuts consent dialog if it appears. If it does not work, add the command shown in Settings, Quick Entry / View as a custom shortcut in your desktop's keyboard settings. See On the desktop guide:desktop global-shortcuts-and-wayland . - No tray icon on GNOME. Install the AppIndicator extension. - Voice input. There is no built-in speech recognizer on Linux. The voice shortcut and \"Say a task\" open the new-task box instead and say that voice input is not available. - Obsidian links are not available on Linux, and browser links need the browser extension. - Updating the AppImage. The AppImage carries update information, so tools such as AppImageUpdate and Gear Lever can update it. It needs no FUSE 2. macOS - Notifications. The first notification asks you to allow Vicu. If you said no, allow it in System Settings. - Microphone and speech recognition. Asked the first time you use voice. Allow both. Apple may send speech to its servers when the language is not recognized on the Mac. Settings, Keyboard Shortcuts says which. - Browser links. On first use, macOS asks you to let Vicu control the browser. - Calendars. Choosing Mac calendars when you add a calendar asks macOS once for access to your calendars. If you refused, allow Vicu in System Settings, under Privacy & Security, and try again. See Events and calendars guide:calendars . Windows The installer is not code-signed, so Windows SmartScreen may say it protected your PC. Choose More info , then Run anyway . Updating keeps Vicu pinned to the taskbar. Where is my data? - Your tasks and most settings are on your Vikunja server. Vicu has no server of its own and collects nothing. No analytics, no ads, no account. - The desktop app keeps a working copy, your device settings, waiting changes and your sign-in in its own folder named vicu : %APPDATA%\\vicu on Windows, ~/Library/Application Support/vicu on macOS and ~/.config/vicu on Linux. The sign-in is encrypted with your system's protection. If your system cannot encrypt it, Settings says so. - The phones keep a copy in the app. Android keeps the sign-in encrypted, and iOS keeps it in the Keychain. - Calendars you connect and the assistant's key stay on the device. Calendar events never go to your server or the assistant. - A deleted Vicu does not delete your tasks. They stay on your server. Report a bug Open an issue and say which app and version you use, what you did, what you expected and what happened. A screenshot helps. - Desktop: github.com/rendyhd/Vicu/issues https://github.com/rendyhd/Vicu/issues - Phone: github.com/rendyhd/Vicu-Mobile/issues https://github.com/rendyhd/Vicu-Mobile/issues Every page of this guide has a link at the bottom to suggest a change. Related - Sync and offline guide:sync - Settings guide:settings - On the desktop guide:desktop - On your phone guide:phone - Glossary guide:glossary"},{"s":"glossary","t":"Glossary","d":"The words Vicu uses, in alphabetical order, with a short definition of each and a link to the page that explains it.","h":[["in-short","In short"],["a","A"],["b","B"],["c","C"],["d","D"],["e","E"],["f","F"],["g","G"],["h","H"],["i","I"],["l","L"],["o","O"],["p","P"],["q","Q"],["r","R"],["s","S"],["t","T"],["u","U"],["v","V"],["w","W"],["related","Related"]],"x":"In short - Terms are in alphabetical order. Terms that belong to Vicu Pro guide:pro are marked . - Missing a word? Open an issue https://github.com/rendyhd/Vicu/issues and we will add it. A - After : A way to say a task waits on other tasks. The row reads \"After: ...\" and the task is not available until they are done. See Know what you can do now guide:pro/available . - Anytime : The smart list of every open task outside the Inbox, with or without a date. See Smart lists guide:lists . - Area : A top-level project that holds other projects below it. The sidebar and the phone's drawer show it as a collapsible group. See Projects and labels guide:projects . - Ask : The assistant's chat. You ask about your tasks, and it answers briefly and proposes changes as cards you apply or decline. See The assistant guide:assistant . - Assistant : An optional helper that uses an AI model you choose. It turns what you say or paste into tasks and answers questions. Off until you set a provider. See The assistant guide:assistant . - Available : A task you can do right now: not waiting for a start date, another task, or an on-hold project. Lists can show Available , Remaining or All . See Know what you can do now guide:pro/available . B - Block : See time block. - Bottom bar : The row of lists at the bottom of the phone app. The Inbox is always first and you pick the other three. See On your phone guide:phone . - Brain dump : Talk or type everything on your mind, and the assistant organizes it into a plan by project, with new projects where none fit. Nothing is added until you accept. See The assistant guide:assistant . C - Cap : The most initiatives you keep active at once, four unless you agree otherwise. Adding one more asks you to park or finish one. See Portfolio guide:pro/portfolio . - Category : An optional named header that groups your top-level projects in the sidebar or drawer. Categories sync to all your devices. See Projects and labels guide:projects . - Command palette : A search box that finds lists, projects, labels and tasks and runs actions. Open it with Ctrl + Shift + P . See On the desktop guide:desktop . - Completion hold : A completed task stays in the list for about 5 seconds, shown as done, before it collapses, so you can undo it. See Work with tasks guide:tasks . - Custom list : A list of your own made from conditions such as project, label, due window and priority. It is stored on your server, so every device shows it. See Custom lists guide:custom-lists . D - Default reminder : A reminder added automatically to every new task that has a due date and no reminder of its own. See Settings guide:settings . - Drawer : The side menu on the phone with your lists, projects and labels. It is the phone's version of the sidebar. See On your phone guide:phone . E - Escalation : A draft message for your sponsor, made when a promise is missed twice or the number you set . You can put it on an agenda, copy it, resolve it or dismiss it. See Portfolio guide:pro/portfolio . - Estimate : How long a task takes, typed as ~30m . Plan my day uses it as the task's length. See Know what you can do now guide:pro/available . - Event : Something on your calendar, as opposed to a task. Your own Events sync between devices and appear on the Plan screen as busy time. Calendars you connect are shown the same way. See Events and calendars guide:calendars . F - Fit : A Plan action that gives tasks without a time the next free slots in your day. See Plan your day guide:plan . - Flag : Marks a task for attention. It is Vikunja's favorite star, so other apps see it. Typing !flag sets it. See Know what you can do now guide:pro/available . - Focus : Shows only one area of your projects everywhere until you leave it. See Know what you can do now guide:pro/available . G - Guided session : A review the app leads one decision at a time: Sort the Inbox, Sort out overdue, Waiting a long time, and Review projects. See Review and guided organizing guide:review . H - Hidden task : A task Vicu marks done and hides everywhere in Vicu. It holds data such as custom lists, routines, synced settings and Pro records. You may see it in other Vikunja apps. Do not delete it. See Sync and offline guide:sync the-hidden-tasks . I - Inbox : Where new tasks land when you add them without choosing a project. You pick which project is the Inbox, and that choice syncs. See How Vicu is organized guide:basics . - Initiative : A project you steer as a portfolio item, with a rank, a readiness check and a sponsor. Start one from the project's menu. See Portfolio guide:pro/portfolio . L - Label : A colored tag you put on tasks. Each label has its own page. See Projects and labels guide:projects . - Logbook : The smart list of finished tasks, grouped by the day you completed them. See Smart lists guide:lists . O - Offline queue : The list of changes made without a connection. They wait and go to the server in order when you are back. See Sync and offline guide:sync the-offline-queue . - On hold : A project or label that Pro treats as paused, so its tasks are not available. See Know what you can do now guide:pro/available . P - Parked : An initiative you set aside with a reason. Its tasks leave Today and Upcoming until you resume it. See Portfolio guide:pro/portfolio . - Person : A label you marked as a person with This is a person . People can owe you promises, and you can owe them. See Promises and people guide:pro/promises . - Plan : The optional screen with a timeline, where you give tasks a time. Off until you turn it on. See Plan your day guide:plan . - Plan my day : A four-step session that takes you from leftover tasks to a planned day: Leftovers, Choose, Arrange and Ready. See Plan your day guide:plan . - Project : A group of tasks. Projects can contain subprojects, be split into sections, and be shared with others. See Projects and labels guide:projects . - Project review : Stepping through your projects on a schedule to keep them current. Each shows when it was last reviewed. See Review and guided organizing guide:review . - Promise : Something a person committed to, or that you committed to them. It has a due date and ends with a verdict. See Promises and people guide:pro/promises . Q - Quick add : Typing a task's title with dates, labels, projects and priority in plain words. See Quick add syntax guide:quick-add . - Quick Entry : A small box that opens over any app from a global shortcut so you can add a task fast. See On the desktop guide:desktop . - Quick find : The search box at the top of the sidebar that matches tasks as you type. See On the desktop guide:desktop . - Quick View : A floating list from a global shortcut where you check off tasks without switching windows. See On the desktop guide:desktop . R - Readiness : A nine-item checklist on an initiative: sponsor, owner, why, what done looks like, and more. Adding a task to an unready initiative asks first. See Portfolio guide:pro/portfolio . - Recap : A text summary of what was agreed with someone, to copy or share. See Promises and people guide:pro/promises . - Review : The list that shows which projects are due for a check. See also project review and weekly review. See Review and guided organizing guide:review . - Routine : A habit that comes back on a schedule, with up to five times a day. A missed one is \"Not logged\", never overdue. See Routines guide:routines . S - Saved filter : A filter you saved in Vikunja. Vicu can import it as a custom list. See Custom lists guide:custom-lists . - Section : A collapsible group of tasks inside a project, with its own header and count. See Projects and labels guide:projects . - Smart dictation : Voice input where you keep talking and the assistant turns each pause into draft task cards. See The assistant guide:assistant . - Smart list : One of Vicu's built-in lists: Inbox, Today, Upcoming, Anytime and Logbook. See Smart lists guide:lists . - Sponsor desk : A desktop screen with escalations, decisions you are owed, initiatives without agreed capacity, and a one-page summary. See Portfolio guide:pro/portfolio . - Standalone mode : Tasks kept only on the computer, without a Vikunja server, as earlier versions allowed. No Plan, time blocks or Pro. See Sync and offline guide:sync standalone-mode . - Start date : The date a task can begin. Until then it is not available. It is Vikunja's own start date. See Know what you can do now guide:pro/available . - Subtask : A task inside another task, shown in the parent's card or under it. See Work with tasks guide:tasks . - Synced setting : A setting stored on your server so it follows you to every device. See Settings guide:settings . T - Task template : A saved task, with its project, notes, labels, dates counted in days from today, reminders and subtasks. Use it to start a new task. See Work with tasks guide:tasks . - Time block : Time you set aside for a task, with a start and an end on its due day. Type at 3pm for 30m to make one. It shows on Today and on Plan. See Plan your day guide:plan . - Today : The smart list of what is due before the start of tomorrow: an Overdue section, today's tasks, tasks whose time block starts today, and today's routines. See Smart lists guide:lists . U - Upcoming : The smart list of everything from tomorrow on, grouped by day. See Smart lists guide:lists . V - Verdict : Your judgment on a promise when it is due: Delivered, Late, Not yet, New date, Dropped or Ask me tomorrow. See Promises and people guide:pro/promises . W - Waiting on : A view of what others owe you, by person. See Promises and people guide:pro/promises . - Weekly review : A checklist for the end of the week, from sorting the Inbox to a look ahead. Finishing it records the date. See Review and guided organizing guide:review . - When panel : The one panel for setting a date and time on a task: type it in words, pick a quick choice, or use the calendar. See Work with tasks guide:tasks . - Work hours : The start and end of your day on Plan. Free time, Fit and Plan my day use them. See Settings guide:settings . Related - How Vicu is organized guide:basics - Quick add syntax guide:quick-add - Vicu Pro guide:pro - Troubleshooting and FAQ guide:faq"}];
